gnature to date. In 2021, NASA announced that pollution could be the key to finding extraterrestrial life. A 2021 study examined the presence of nitrogen dioxide in the atmosphere. On Earth, nitrogen dioxide is produced by burning fossil fuels, biology, lightning strikes, and volcanos. However, the majority of nitrogen dioxide is caused by human activity. Therefore, observing nitrogen dioxide on a habitable planet could indicate the presence of an advanced civilization. Researchers believe that if they observe more nitrogen dioxide than could be generated from non-industrial sources, some nitrogen dioxide may have stemmed from industrial activities. While they recognize that a false positive is possible, they hope to learn in the future how to discern positives from false positives. According to a study conducted in 2022, scientists searching for the possibility of extraterrestrial life should be on the lookout for city lights on exoplanets. More urbanized planets would have higher nightside brightness from city lights. Researchers who conducted the study also urged the astronomical community to think more about the possibility of detecting technosignatures using a community-wide effort. They felt that searching for technosignatures is a logical extension when searching for extraterrestrial life through biosignatures. They pointed out that the idea of searching for technosignatures has been considered by astronomers for more than half a century, with initial efforts focused on the possibility of detecting extraterrestrial radio transmissions. Those in the SETI community believe that technosignatures could be more abundant, longer lasting, and easier to detect than biosignatures, yet the search for biosignatures receives more funding. Researchers concluded during a second study in 2022 that the search for biosignatures should include the search for technosignatures because the two complement and supplement each other. Both are indicative of life outside of that on Earth. According to an article on the website *Space.com* in 2023, scientists had begun using machine learning, a type of artificial intelligence, to detect technosignatures. The scientists used new computer algorithms to analyze data that had been collected by the Green Bank Telescope in West Virginia to find signals that might be coming from advanced extraterrestrial civilizations. These new algorithms were able to organize the data from the telescope into categories, so they could distinguish between actual signals and interference. They discovered eight signals that resembled potential technosignatures. However, they need to detect the same signals multiple times to confirm that they originated from extraterrestial intelligence. ### Bibliography David, Leonard. “Alien Hunters Should Look for City Lights from 'Urbanized Planets,' Study Suggests.” *Space.com*, 18 Oct. 2018, www.space.com/alien-life-seach-city-lights-exoplanets. Accessed 22 Aug. 2022. Davis, Jason. “Aliens at Proxima Centauri? A New Radio Signal Raises the Question.” *The Planetary Society*, 22 Dec. 2020, www.planetary.org/articles/aliens-at-proxima-centauri-a-new-radio-signal-raises-the-question. Accessed 22 Aug. 2022. Kaufman, Marc. “Technosignatures and the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence.” *Astrobiology at NASA*, July 2022, astrobiology.nasa.gov/news/technosignatures-and-the-search-for-extraterrestrial-intelligence/. Accessed 22 Aug. 2022. Lea, Robert. \"Machine Learning Spots Eight Possible Technosignatures.\" *Space.com*, 31 Jan. 2023, www.space.com/machine-learning-seti-technosignatures. Accessed 8 Nov. 2024. Powell, Corey S. “The Search for Alien Tech.” *Aeon*, 25 Oct. 2021, aeon.co/essays/technosignatures-are-a-sea-change-in-the-search-for-alien-life. Accessed 22 Aug. 2022. Tarter, J.C. & Gulkis, S. “The NASA High Resolution Microwave Survey.” Bulletin of the *American Astrological Society*, May 1993, ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1993AAS...182.7101T/abstract. Accessed 22 Aug. 2022.\n\n### 3. A Deep SETI Search For Technosignatures In The TRAPPIST-1 System With FAST - Astrobiology\n- **URL**: https://astrobiology.com/2025/09/10/a-deep-seti-search-for-technosignatures-in-the-trappist-1-system-with-fast\n- [Skip to content](#main) Posted in[Astronomy & Telescopes](https://astrobiology.com/category/astronomy/), [Biosignatures & Paleobiology](https://astrobiology.com/category/paleobiology-biosignatures/), [Exoplanets, -moons, -comets](https://astrobiology.com/category/extrasolar-planets/), [Intelligence & Language](https://astrobiology.com/category/intelligence-language/), [Interstellar](https://astrobiology.com/category/interstellar/), [SETI & Technosignatures](https://astrobiology.com/category/seti/), [Status Report](https://astrobiology.com/category/narrow-angle-camera/), [TRAPPIST-1](https://astrobiology.com/category/trappist-1/) # A Deep SETI Search For Technosignatures In The TRAPPIST-1 System With FAST ![](https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/265c1ec3d6dc55473abfd76e0fc0a50e6e55af16308beaaee9a756b1d4f4fbd6?s=96&d=mm&r=g)by [Keith Cowing](https://astrobiology.com/author/keith_cowing/) ### Share this: * [Share on Bluesky (Opens in new window) Bluesky](https://astrobiology.com/2025/09/10/a-deep-seti-search-for-technosignatures-in-the-trappist-1-system-with-fast/?share=bluesky&nb=1) * [Share on Facebook (Opens in new window) Facebook](https://astrobiology.com/2025/09/10/a-deep-seti-search-for-technosignatures-in-the-trappist-1-system-with-fast/?share=facebook&nb=1) * [Share on X (Opens in new window) X](https://astrobiology.com/2025/09/10/a-deep-seti-search-for-technosignatures-in-the-trappist-1-system-with-fast/?share=x&nb=1) * [Share on LinkedIn (Opens in new window) LinkedIn](https://astrobiology.com/2025/09/10/a-deep-seti-search-for-technosignatures-in-the-trappist-1-system-with-fast/?share=linkedin&nb=1) ![Schematic of the TRAPPIST-1 planetary system. The seven known planets are shown orbiting the central star. The semi-transparent green annulus marks the optimistic habitable zone, which contains planets e, f, and g the primary targets of this search. -- astro-ph.IM](https://i0.wp.com/astrobiology.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/A-Deep-SETI-Search-For.png?fit=1200%2C676&ssl=1) Schematic of the TRAPPIST-1 planetary system. The seven known planets are shown orbiting the central star. The semi-transparent green annulus marks the optimistic habitable zone, which contains planets e, f, and g the primary targets of this search. -- astro-ph.IM The Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical Telescope (FAST) is the world’s largest single-dish radio telescope, and the search for extraterrestrial intelligence (SETI) is one of its five key science objectives. We conducted a targeted narrowband search toward the TRAPPIST-1 system using FAST. The observations consisted of five independent L-band pointings, each with a 20-minute integration, for a total on-source time of 1.67h. The frequency coverage spanned 1.05–1.45GHz with a spectral resolution of ~7.5Hz. We searched for narrowband drifting signals with Doppler drift rates within +\\_4Hz/s and a signal-to-noise ratio threshold of S/N>10 in two orthogonal linear polarizations this http URL on the system parameters adopted in this work, we estimate a minimum detectable equivalent isotropic radiated power of 2.04×10^10W, placing one of the most stringent constraints to date on persistent or high-duty-cycle narrowband transmitters in this system. No credible technosignature candidates were identified within the searched parameter space. Nevertheless,TRAPPIST-1 remains a compelling target for future SETI efforts. We plan to extend our search to other signal types, such as periodic or transient transmitters, and to carry out broader surveys of nearby exoplanetary systems with FAST. Guang-Yuan Song, Zhen-Zhao Tao, Bo-Lun Huang, Yan Cui, Bo Yu, Tong-Jie Zhang Comments: 10 pages, 5 figures, Submitted to AJ Subjects: Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM); Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP) Cite as: arXiv:2509.06310 [astro-ph.IM](or arXiv:2509.06310v1 [astro-ph.IM] for this version) Focus to learn more Submission history From: Zhenzhao Tao [v1] Mon, 8 Sep 2025 03:23:39 UTC (1,380 KB) Astrobiology ### *Related* [![](https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/265c1ec3d6dc55473abfd76e0fc0a50e6e55af16308beaaee9a756b1d4f4fbd6?s=120&d=mm&r=g)](https://astrobiology.com/author/keith_cowing/) ## [Keith Cowing](https://astrobiology.com/author/keith_cowing/) Explorers Club Fellow, ex-NASA Space Station Payload manager/space biologist, Away Teams, Journalist, Lapsed climber, Synaesthete, Na’Vi-Jedi-Freman-Buddhist-mix, ASL, Devon Island and Everest Base Camp... [More by Keith Cowing](https://astrobiology.com/author/keith_cowing/) ## Gift this article\n\n### 4. Technosignature - Wikipedia\n- **URL**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technosignature\n- [Jump to content](#bodyContent) [![](/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg) ![Wikipedia](/static/images/mobile/copyright/wikipedia-wordmark-en-25.svg) ![The Free Encyclopedia](/static/images/mobile/copyright/wikipedia-tagline-en-25.svg)](/wiki/Main_Page) [Search](/wiki/Special:Search \"Search Wikipedia [f]\") ## Contents * [1 Astroengineering projects](#Astroengineering_projects) + [1.1 Orbital paths, transit signatures, stellar activity and star-system composition](#Orbital_paths,_transit_signatures,_stellar_activity_and_star-system_composition) + [1.2 Communication networks](#Communication_networks) * [2 Planetary analysis](#Planetary_analysis) + [2.1 Artificial heat and light](#Artificial_heat_and_light) + [2.2 Atmospheric analysis](#Atmospheric_analysis) * [3 Extraterrestrial artifacts and spacecraft](#Extraterrestrial_artifacts_and_spacecraft) + [3.1 Spacecraft](#Spacecraft) + [3.2 Satellites](#Satellites) + [3.3 On exoplanets](#On_exoplanets) * [4 Scientific projects searching for technosignatures](#Scientific_projects_searching_for_technosignatures) + [4.1 Vanishing stars](#Vanishing_stars) + [4.2 Organization of novel projects](#Organization_of_novel_projects) * [5 Implications of detection](#Implications_of_detection) * [6 See also](#See_also) * [7 Further reading](#Further_reading) * [8 References](#References) # Technosignature * [العربية](https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D9%88%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%B9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D9%83%D9%86%D9%88%D9%84%D9%88%D8%AC%D9%8A \"التوقيع التكنولوجي – Arabic\") * [Deutsch](https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technosignatur \"Technosignatur – German\") * [Esperanto](https://eo.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teknosignaturo \"Teknosignaturo – Esperanto\") * [Español](https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tecnomarcador \"Tecnomarcador – Spanish\") * [فارسی](https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B1%D8%AF%D9%BE%D8%A7%DB%8C_%D9%81%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%88%D8%B1%DB%8C \"ردپای فناوری – Persian\") * [Français](https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technosignature \"Technosignature – French\") * [Hausa](https://ha.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technosignature \"Technosignature – Hausa\") * [עברית](https://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D7%97%D7%AA%D7%99%D7%9E%D7%94_%D7%98%D7%9B%D7%A0%D7%95%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%92%D7%99%D7%AA \"חתימה טכנולוגית – Hebrew\") * [Português](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tecnoassinatura \"Tecnoassinatura – Portuguese\") * [Русский](https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A2%D0%B5%D1%85%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B3%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B0 \"Техносигнатура – Russian\") * [Українська](https://uk.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A2%D0%B5%D1%85%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B3%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B0 \"Техносигнатура – Ukrainian\") [Edit links](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:EntityPage/Q17156964#sitelinks-wikipedia \"Edit interlanguage links\") * [Article](/wiki/Technosignature \"View the content page [c]\") * [Talk](/wiki/Talk:Technosignature \"Discuss improvements to the content page [t]\") * [Read](/wiki/Technosignature) * [Edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit \"Edit this page [e]\") * [View history](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=history \"Past revisions of this page [h]\") Tools Actions * [Read](/wiki/Technosignature) * [Edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit \"Edit this page [e]\") * [View history](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=history \"Past revisions of this page [h]\") General * [What links here](/wiki/Special:WhatLinksHere/Technosignature \"List of all English Wikipedia pages containing links to this page [j]\") * [Related changes](/wiki/Special:RecentChangesLinked/Technosignature \"Recent changes in pages linked from this page [k]\") * [Upload file](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:File_Upload_Wizard \"Upload files [u]\") * [Permanent link](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&oldid=1367304957 \"Permanent link to this revision of this page\") * [Page information](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=info \"More information about this page\") * [Cite this page](/w/index.php?title=Special:CiteThisPage&page=Technosignature&id=1367304957&wpFormIdentifier=titleform \"Information on how to cite this page\") * [Get shortened URL](/w/index.php?title=Special:UrlShortener&url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FTechnosignature) * [Switch to legacy parser](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&useparsoid=0) Print/export * [Download as PDF](/w/index.php?title=Special:DownloadAsPdf&page=Technosignature&action=show-download-screen \"Download this page as a PDF file\") * [Printable version](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&printable=yes \"Printable version of this page [p]\") In other projects * [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Technosignatures) * [Wikidata item](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:EntityPage/Q17156964 \"Structured data on this page hosted by Wikidata [g]\") Appearance From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Property that provides scientific evidence for the presence of technology [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Technosignatures.jpg/500px-Technosignatures.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Technosignatures.jpg) Illustration of various types of technosignatures **Technosignature** or **technomarker** is any measurable property or effect that provides [scientific evidence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_evidence \"Scientific evidence\") of past or [present technology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_emerging_technologies \"List of emerging technologies\").[[1]](#cite_note-tarter-2018-1)[[2]](#cite_note-UT-2018-2) Technosignatures are analogous to [biosignatures](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biosignatures \"Biosignatures\"), which signal the presence of life, whether intelligent or not.[[1]](#cite_note-tarter-2018-1)[[3]](#cite_note-WP-20201231-3) Some authors prefer to exclude radio transmissions from the definition,[[4]](#cite_note-almar-2011-4) but such restrictive usage is not widespread. [Jill Tarter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jill_Tarter \"Jill Tarter\") has proposed that the [search for extraterrestrial intelligence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Search_for_extraterrestrial_intelligence \"Search for extraterrestrial intelligence\") (SETI) be renamed \"the search for technosignatures\".[[1]](#cite_note-tarter-2018-1) Various types of technosignatures, such as radiation leakage from [megascale](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megascale_engineering \"Megascale engineering\") [astroengineering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astroengineering \"Astroengineering\") installations such as [Dyson spheres](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyson_sphere \"Dyson sphere\"), the light from an extraterrestrial [ecumenopolis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecumenopolis \"Ecumenopolis\"), or [Shkadov thrusters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shkadov_thrusters \"Shkadov thrusters\") with the power to alter the orbits of stars around the [Galactic Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galactic_Center \"Galactic Center\"), may be detectable with [hypertelescopes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertelescope \"Hypertelescope\"). Some examples of technosignatures are described in [Paul Davies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Davies \"Paul Davies\")'s 2010 book *[The Eerie Silence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Eerie_Silence \"The Eerie Silence\")*, although the terms \"technosignature\" and \"technomarker\" do not appear in the book. In February 2023, astronomers reported, after scanning 820 stars, the detection of 8 possible technosignatures for follow-up studies.[[5]](#cite_note-NA-20221130-5) ## Astroengineering projects [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: Astroengineering projects\")] [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Dyson_Sphere_Render.png/250px-Dyson_Sphere_Render.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dyson_Sphere_Render.png) A [Dyson sphere](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyson_sphere \"Dyson sphere\"), one of the best-known speculative technologies that may generate a technosignature A [Dyson sphere](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyson_sphere \"Dyson sphere\"), constructed by life forms dwelling in proximity to a [Sun-like star](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_analog \"Solar analog\"), would cause an increase in the amount of infrared radiation in the star system's emitted spectrum. Hence, [Freeman Dyson](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freeman_Dyson \"Freeman Dyson\") selected the title \"Search for Artificial Stellar Sources of Infrared Radiation\" for his 1960 paper on the subject.[[6]](#cite_note-search-6) SETI has adopted these assumptions in its search, looking for such \"infrared heavy\" spectra from [solar analogs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_analog \"Solar analog\"). Since 2005, [Fermilab](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermilab \"Fermilab\") has conducted an ongoing survey for such spectra, analyzing data from the [Infrared Astronomical Satellite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infrared_Astronomical_Satellite \"Infrared Astronomical Satellite\").[[7]](#cite_note-carrigan-7)[[8]](#cite_note-8) Identifying one of the many infra-red sources as a Dyson sphere would require improved techniques for discriminating between a Dyson sphere and natural sources.[[9]](#cite_note-9) Fermilab discovered 17 \"ambiguous\" candidates, of which four have been named \"amusing but still questionable\".[[10]](#cite_note-Carrigan_2012-10) Other searches also resulted in several candidates, which remain unconfirmed.[[7]](#cite_note-carrigan-7) In October 2012, astronomer [Geoff Marcy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoff_Marcy \"Geoff Marcy\"), one of the pioneers of the search for [extrasolar planets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extrasolar_planets \"Extrasolar planets\"), was given a research grant to search data from the *[Kepler](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kepler_(spacecraft) \"Kepler (spacecraft)\")* telescope, with the aim of detecting possible signs of Dyson spheres.[[11]](#cite_note-11) ### Orbital paths, transit signatures, stellar activity and star-system composition [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Orbital paths, transit signatures, stellar activity and star-system composition\")] See also: [Moving Earth](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moving_Earth \"Moving Earth\") and [Technosignature § Extraterrestrial artifacts and spacecraft](#Extraterrestrial_artifacts_and_spacecraft) Shkadov thrusters, with the hypothetical ability to change the orbital paths of stars in order to avoid various dangers to life such as [cold molecular clouds](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_cloud \"Molecular cloud\") or [cometary impacts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impact_event \"Impact event\"), would also be detectable in a similar fashion to the [transiting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transit_method \"Transit method\") extrasolar planets searched by *Kepler*. Unlike planets, though, the thrusters would appear to abruptly stop over the surface of a star rather than crossing it completely, revealing their technological origin.[[12]](#cite_note-Villard2013-12) In addition, evidence of targeted extrasolar [asteroid mining](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asteroid_mining \"Asteroid mining\") may also reveal [extraterrestrial intelligence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extraterrestrial_intelligence \"Extraterrestrial intelligence\") (ETI).[[13]](#cite_note-13) Furthermore, it has been suggested that information could be hidden within the transit signatures of other planets.[[14]](#cite_note-14) Advanced civilizations could \"cloak their presence, or deliberately broadcast it, through controlled laser emission\".[[15]](#cite_note-15) Other characteristics proposed as potential technosignatures (or starting points for detection of clearer signatures) include peculiar [orbital periods](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orbital_period \"Orbital period\") such as arranging planets in prime number patterns.[[16]](#cite_note-16)[[17]](#cite_note-17)[[18]](#cite_note-18) Coronal and [chromospheric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chromosphere \"Chromosphere\") activity on stars might be altered.[[19]](#cite_note-19) Extraterrestrial civilizations may use free-floating planets ([rogue planets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rogue_planet \"Rogue planet\")) for interstellar transportation with a number of proposed possible technosignatures.[[20]](#cite_note-20) ### Communication networks [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Communication networks\")] See also: [Search for extraterrestrial intelligence § Quantum communications](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Search_for_extraterrestrial_intelligence#Quantum_communications \"Search for extraterrestrial intelligence\") A study suggests that if ETs exist, they may have established communications network(s) and may already have probes in the [Solar System](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_System \"Solar System\") whose communication may be detectable.[[21]](#cite_note-21) Studies by John Gertz suggest flyby (scout)[[22]](#cite_note-22) probes might intermittently surveil nascent planetary systems and permanent probes would communicate with a home base, potentially using triggers and conditions such as detection of electromagnetic leakage or biosignatures.[[23]](#cite_note-23) They also suggest several strategies to detecting local ET probes[[24]](#cite_note-24) such as detecting emitted optical messages.[[25]](#cite_note-25) He also finds that due to interstellar networks of communications nodes, the search for deliberate interstellar signals – as is common in SETI[[26]](#cite_note-10.3847/1538-3881/ab2df3-26) – may be futile.[[27]](#cite_note-27) The architecture may consist of nodes separated by sub-light-year distances and strung out between neighboring stars.[[28]](#cite_note-28) It may also contain pulsars as [beacons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beacon \"Beacon\")[[29]](#cite_note-29) or nodes whose beams are modulated by mechanisms that could be searched for.[[30]](#cite_note-30) Moreover, a study suggests prior searches wouldn't have detected cost-effective electromagnetic signal beacons.[[31]](#cite_note-31) ## Planetary analysis [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: Planetary analysis\")] ### Artificial heat and light [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: Artificial heat and light\")] [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Earth%27s_City_Lights_by_DMSP%2C_1994-1995_%28large%29.jpg/250px-Earth%27s_City_Lights_by_DMSP%2C_1994-1995_%28large%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Earth's_City_Lights_by_DMSP,_1994-1995_(large).jpg) Lights from cities and infrastructure on Earth at night from space Various astronomers, including [Avi Loeb](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avi_Loeb \"Avi Loeb\") of the [Harvard-Smithsonian Center for Astrophysics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard-Smithsonian_Center_for_Astrophysics \"Harvard-Smithsonian Center for Astrophysics\"). [Edwin L. Turner](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edwin_L._Turner?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Edwin L. Turner (page does not exist)\") of [Princeton University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princeton_University \"Princeton University\"), and [Thomas Beatty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Beatty?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Thomas Beatty (page does not exist)\") of the [University of Wisconsin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Wisconsin–Madison \"University of Wisconsin–Madison\") have proposed that artificial light from extraterrestrial planets, such as that originating from cities, industries, and transport networks, could be detected and signal the presence of an advanced civilization. Light and heat detected from planets must be distinguished from natural sources to conclusively prove the existence of intelligent life on a planet.[[4]](#cite_note-almar-2011-4) For example, NASA's 2012 *Black Marble* experiment showed that significant stable light and heat sources on Earth, such as chronic wildfires in arid [Western Australia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Australia \"Western Australia\"), originate from uninhabited areas and are naturally occurring.[[32]](#cite_note-32) Spectroscopic observations of exoplanet nightsides would be able to identify artificial lighting via its distinct spectroscopic signature. Work by astronomer [Thomas Beatty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Beatty?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Thomas Beatty (page does not exist)\") has shown that the spectrally concentrated emission from [sodium street lights](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium-vapor_lamp \"Sodium-vapor lamp\") would be distinguishable from natural sources using proposed next generation space telescopes. The proposed [Large Ultraviolet Optical Infrared Surveyor](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_Ultraviolet_Optical_Infrared_Surveyor \"Large Ultraviolet Optical Infrared Surveyor\") may be able to detect city lights twelve times those of Earth on [Proxima b](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proxima_b \"Proxima b\") in 300 hours.[[33]](#cite_note-33) ### Atmospheric analysis [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=6 \"Edit section: Atmospheric analysis\")] [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Artist%E2%80%99s_illustration_of_an_advanced_extraterrestrial_civilization_with_industrial_pollution%2C_a_possible_%22technosignature%22.jpg/250px-Artist%E2%80%99s_illustration_of_an_advanced_extraterrestrial_civilization_with_industrial_pollution%2C_a_possible_%22technosignature%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Artist’s_illustration_of_an_advanced_extraterrestrial_civilization_with_industrial_pollution,_a_possible_) Artist's illustration of an advanced ET civilization with industrial pollution[[34]](#cite_note-34) Atmospheric analysis of planetary atmospheres, as is already done on various Solar System bodies and in a rudimentary fashion on several [hot Jupiter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Jupiter \"Hot Jupiter\") extrasolar planets, may reveal the presence of chemicals produced by technological civilizations.[[35]](#cite_note-NYT-20220915-35)[[36]](#cite_note-choi-2012-36) For example, atmospheric emissions from human technology use on Earth, including [nitrogen dioxide](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrogen_dioxide \"Nitrogen dioxide\") and [chlorofluorocarbons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chlorofluorocarbon \"Chlorofluorocarbon\"), are detectable from space.[[37]](#cite_note-37) Artificial air pollution may therefore be detectable on extrasolar planets and on Earth via \"atmospheric SETI\" – including NO2 pollution levels and with telescopic technology close to today.[[38]](#cite_note-38)[[39]](#cite_note-39)[[40]](#cite_note-40)[[41]](#cite_note-41)[*[excessive citations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citing_sources#Bundling_citations \"Wikipedia:Citing sources\")*] Such technosignatures may consist not of the detection of the level of one specific chemical but simultaneous detections of levels of multiple specific chemicals in atmospheres.[[42]](#cite_note-42) However, there remains a possibility of mis-detection; for example, the [atmosphere of Titan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmosphere_of_Titan \"Atmosphere of Titan\") has detectable signatures of complex chemicals that are similar to what on Earth are industrial pollutants, though not the byproduct of civilisation.[[43]](#cite_note-43) Some SETI scientists have proposed searching for artificial atmospheres created by planetary engineering to produce habitable environments for colonisation by an ETI.[[36]](#cite_note-choi-2012-36) ## Extraterrestrial artifacts and spacecraft [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=7 \"Edit section: Extraterrestrial artifacts and spacecraft\")] ### Spacecraft [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: Spacecraft\")] See also: [Fast radio burst](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fast_radio_burst \"Fast radio burst\") [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/IKAROS_solar_sail.jpg/250px-IKAROS_solar_sail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:IKAROS_solar_sail.jpg) The [IKAROS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IKAROS \"IKAROS\") [light sail](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_sail \"Solar sail\") of 2010 [Interstellar spacecraft](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interstellar_spacecraft \"Interstellar spacecraft\") may be detectable from hundreds to thousands of [light-years](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light-year \"Light-year\") away through various forms of radiation, such as the [photons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photon \"Photon\") emitted by an [antimatter rocket](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antimatter_rocket \"Antimatter rocket\") or [cyclotron radiation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclotron_radiation \"Cyclotron radiation\") from the interaction of a magnetic sail with the [interstellar medium](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interstellar_medium \"Interstellar medium\"). Such a signal would be easily distinguishable from a natural signal and could hence firmly establish the existence of extraterrestrial life, were it to be detected.[[44]](#cite_note-44) In addition, smaller [Bracewell probes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bracewell_probe \"Bracewell probe\") within the Solar System itself may also be detectable by means of optical or radio searches.[[45]](#cite_note-freitas-1983-45)[[46]](#cite_note-tough-1998-2-46) [Self-replicating spacecraft](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-replicating_spacecraft \"Self-replicating spacecraft\") or their communications networks could potentially be detectable within the Solar system or in nearby star-based systems,[[47]](#cite_note-47) if they are located there.[[48]](#cite_note-48) ### Satellites [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=9 \"Edit section: Satellites\")] A less advanced technology, and one closer to humanity's current technological level, is the Clarke Exobelt proposed by Astrophysicist Hector Socas-Navarro of the [Instituto de Astrofisica de Canarias](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instituto_de_Astrofisica_de_Canarias \"Instituto de Astrofisica de Canarias\").[[49]](#cite_note-49) This hypothetical belt would be formed by all the artificial [satellites](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite \"Satellite\") occupying [geostationary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geostationary \"Geostationary\")/[geosynchronous](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geosynchronous \"Geosynchronous\") orbits around an [exoplanet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exoplanet \"Exoplanet\"). From early simulations it appeared that a very dense satellite belt, requiring only a moderately more-advanced civilization than ours, would be detectable with existing technology in the [light curves](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light_curve \"Light curve\") from [transiting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Methods_of_detecting_exoplanets#Transit_photometry \"Methods of detecting exoplanets\") exoplanets,[[50]](#cite_note-50) but subsequent analysis has questioned this result, suggesting that exobelts detectable by current and upcoming missions will be very rare.[[51]](#cite_note-51) ### On exoplanets [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=10 \"Edit section: On exoplanets\")] Low- or high-albedo installations such as solar panels may also be detectable, albeit distinguishing artificial megastructures from high- and low-albedo natural environments (e.g., bright ice caps) may make it unfeasible.[[26]](#cite_note-10.3847/1538-3881/ab2df3-26) ## Scientific projects searching for technosignatures [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=11 \"Edit section: Scientific projects searching for technosignatures\")] [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Technosignatures_chart_%28labelled%29.png/500px-Technosignatures_chart_%28labelled%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Technosignatures_chart_(labelled).png) Major technosignatures as outlined in a 2021 scientific review[[52]](#cite_note-10.1016/j.actaastro.2021.02.029-52) One of the first attempts to search for Dyson Spheres was made by Vyacheslav Slysh from the [Russian Space Research Institute](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_Space_Research_Institute \"Russian Space Research Institute\") in Moscow in 1985 using data from the [Infrared Astronomical Satellite (IRAS)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRAS \"IRAS\").[[53]](#cite_note-53) Another search for technosignatures, c. 2001, involved an analysis of data from the [Compton Gamma Ray Observatory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compton_Gamma_Ray_Observatory \"Compton Gamma Ray Observatory\") for traces of anti-matter, which, besides one \"intriguing spectrum probably not related to SETI\", came up empty.[[54]](#cite_note-harris-2001-54) In 2005, [Fermilab](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermilab \"Fermilab\") had an ongoing survey for such spectra by analyzing data from IRAS.[[55]](#cite_note-55)[[56]](#cite_note-56) Identifying one of the many infra-red sources as a Dyson Sphere would require improved techniques for discriminating between a Dyson Sphere and natural sources.[[57]](#cite_note-57) Fermilab discovered 17 potential \"ambiguous\" candidates of which four have been named \"amusing but still questionable\".[[10]](#cite_note-Carrigan_2012-10) Other searches also resulted in several candidates, which are, however, unconfirmed.[[58]](#cite_note-58) In a 2005 paper, Luc Arnold proposed a means of detecting planetary-sized artifacts from their distinctive transit light curve signature. He showed that such technosignature was within the reach of space missions aimed at detecting exoplanets by the [transit method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transit_method \"Transit method\"), as were *Corot* or *Kepler* projects at that time.[[59]](#cite_note-59) The principle of the detection remains applicable for future exoplanets missions.[[60]](#cite_note-60)[[61]](#cite_note-61)[[62]](#cite_note-62) In 2012, a trio of astronomers led by Jason Wright started a two-year search for Dyson Spheres, aided by grants from the [Templeton Foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templeton_Foundation \"Templeton Foundation\").[[63]](#cite_note-UT-2013-63) In 2013, Geoff Marcy received funding to use data from the [Kepler Telescope](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kepler_Telescope \"Kepler Telescope\") to search for Dyson Spheres and interstellar communication using lasers,[[64]](#cite_note-SMH-2013-64) and [Lucianne Walkowicz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucianne_Walkowicz \"Lucianne Walkowicz\") received funding to detect artificial signatures in stellar photometry.[[65]](#cite_note-65) Starting in 2016, astronomer [Jean-Luc Margot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Luc_Margot \"Jean-Luc Margot\") of [UCLA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UCLA \"UCLA\") has been searching for technosignatures with large radio telescopes.[[2]](#cite_note-UT-2018-2) ### Vanishing stars [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=12 \"Edit section: Vanishing stars\")] In 2016, it was proposed that vanishing stars are a plausible technosignature.[[66]](#cite_note-Villarroel_et_al_2016-66) A pilot project searching for vanishing stars was carried out, finding one candidate object. In 2019, the Vanishing & Appearing Sources during a Century of Observations (VASCO) project[[67]](#cite_note-67) began more general searches for vanishing and appearing stars, and other [astrophysical transients](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time-domain_astronomy \"Time-domain astronomy\")[[66]](#cite_note-Villarroel_et_al_2016-66) They identified 100 red transients of \"most likely natural origin\", while analyzing 15% of the image data. In 2020, the VASCO collaboration started up a [citizen science](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citizen_science \"Citizen science\") project, vetting through images of many thousands of candidate objects.[[68]](#cite_note-68) The citizen science project is carried out in close collaboration with schools and amateur associations mainly in African countries.[[69]](#cite_note-69) The VASCO project has been referred to as \"Perhaps the most general artefact search to date\".[[70]](#cite_note-70) In 2021, VASCO's principal investigator Beatriz Villarroel received a [L'Oreal-Unesco prize](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L'Oréal-UNESCO_For_Women_in_Science_Awards \"L'Oréal-UNESCO For Women in Science Awards\") in Sweden for the project.[[71]](#cite_note-71) In June 2021, the collaboration published the discovery of nine light sources seemingly appearing and vanishing simultaneously from archival plates taken in 1950.[[72]](#cite_note-72) Villarroel's team also found three 16th magnitude stars which had vanished on plates exposed within one hour of each other on 19 July 1952.[[73]](#cite_note-73) ### Organization of novel projects [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=13 \"Edit section: Organization of novel projects\")] [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Table_of_technosignatures.png/250px-Table_of_technosignatures.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Table_of_technosignatures.png) Methods and ancillary benefits of the search for various technosignatures[[52]](#cite_note-10.1016/j.actaastro.2021.02.029-52) In June 2020, NASA was awarded their first [SETI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SETI \"SETI\")-specific grant in three decades. The grant funds the first NASA-funded search for technosignatures from advanced extraterrestrial civilizations other than radio waves, including the creation and population of an online technosignature [library](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_library \"Digital library\").[[74]](#cite_note-74)[[75]](#cite_note-75)[[76]](#cite_note-NASA-technosignatures-76) A 2021 [scientific review](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_review \"Scientific review\") produced by the i.a. NASA-sponsored [online workshop](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_event \"Virtual event\") *TechnoClimes 2020* classified possible optimal mission concepts for the search of technosignatures. It evaluates signatures based on a metric about the distance of humanity to the capacity of developing the signature's required technology – a comparison to contemporary human technology footprints, associated methods of detection and ancillary benefits of their search for other astronomy. The study's conclusions include a robust rationale for organizing missions for searching artifacts – including probes – within the Solar system.[[77]](#cite_note-77)[[52]](#cite_note-10.1016/j.actaastro.2021.02.029-52) In 2021, astronomers proposed a sequence of \"verification checks for narrowband technosignature signals\" after concluding that technosignature candidate [BLC1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BLC1 \"BLC1\") could be the result of a form of local [radiofrequency interference](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radiofrequency_interference \"Radiofrequency interference\").[[78]](#cite_note-78) [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Capabilities_for_detecting_technosignatures_with_recent%2C_ongoing%2C_and_future_missions_and_facilities.jpg/250px-Capabilities_for_detecting_technosignatures_with_recent%2C_ongoing%2C_and_future_missions_and_facilities.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Capabilities_for_detecting_technosignatures_with_recent,_ongoing,_and_future_missions_and_facilities.jpg) Capabilities for detecting technosignatures with recent, ongoing, and future missions and facilities. Cells colored green indicate that at least such a signature could be detectable for at least one stellar system and there being at least one peer-reviewed publication that has evaluated detectability of that signature. It has been suggested that observatories on the far side of the Moon may mitigate radio interference from Earth.[[79]](#cite_note-79)[[80]](#cite_note-80) In 2022, scientists provided an overview of the capabilities of ongoing, recent, past, planned and proposed missions and observatories for detecting various alien technosignatures.[[81]](#cite_note-81)[[82]](#cite_note-82) ## Implications of detection [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=14 \"Edit section: Implications of detection\")] See also: [Potential cultural impact of extraterrestrial contact](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potential_cultural_impact_of_extraterrestrial_contact \"Potential cultural impact of extraterrestrial contact\") [Steven J. Dick](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steven_J._Dick \"Steven J. Dick\") states that there generally are no principles for dealing with successful SETI detections. Detections of technosignatures may have ethical implications, such as conveying information related to [astroethical](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astroethics \"Astroethics\")[[83]](#cite_note-83) and related [machine ethics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_ethics \"Machine ethics\") ones (e.g., related to machines' applied ethical [values](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Value_(ethics) \"Value (ethics)\")), or include information about alien [societies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technology_and_society \"Technology and society\") or [histories or fates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xenoarchaeology \"Xenoarchaeology\"), which may vary depending on the type, prevalence and form of the detected signature's technology. Moreover, various types of information about detected technosignatures and their distribution or dissemination may have varying implications that may also depend on time and context. Beyond astronomy and [astrobiology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astrobiology \"Astrobiology\"), detection would raise significant methodological questions about how we search for life. Research into detection windows suggests that advancing technology on both sides; human and extraterrestrial, could so rapidly evolve that the period during which their technosignatures are visible to us might be brief, potentially explaining why [prior searches](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_extraterrestrial_life_debate \"History of the extraterrestrial life debate\") have come up empty and indicating the need for broader, multi-wavelength detection strategies.[[84]](#cite_note-84) ## See also [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=15 \"Edit section: See also\")] * [Laser SETI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laser_SETI \"Laser SETI\") * [Quiet and loud aliens](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quiet_and_loud_aliens \"Quiet and loud aliens\") – Concept in astrobiology * [Kardashev scale](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kardashev_scale \"Kardashev scale\") – Measure of a civilization's evolution ## Further reading [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=16 \"Edit section: Further reading\")] * Participants, NASA Technosignatures Workshop (28 January 2019). *NASA and the Search for Technosignatures: A Report from the NASA Technosignatures Workshop*. [arXiv](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ArXiv_(identifier) \"ArXiv (identifier)\"):[1812.08681v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.08681v2). ## References [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Technosignature&action=edit&section=17 \"Edit section: References\")] 1. [1](#cite_ref-tarter-2018_1-0) [2](#cite_ref-tarter-2018_1-1) [3](#cite_ref-tarter-2018_1-2) [\"'Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence' Needs a New Name, SETI Pioneer Says\"](https://www.space.com/39474-search-for-extraterrestrial-intelligence-needs-new-name.html). *Space.com*. 25 January 2018. 2. [1](#cite_ref-UT-2018_2-0) [2](#cite_ref-UT-2018_2-1) Williams, Matt (9 February 2018). [\"Researchers Just Scanned 14 Worlds From the Kepler Mission for \"Technosignatures\", Evidence of Advanced Civilizations\"](https://www.universetoday.com/138516/researchers-just-scanned-14-worlds-kepler-mission-technosignatures-evidence-advanced-civilizations/). *Universe Today*. Retrieved 2018-02-13. 3. [↑](#cite_ref-WP-20201231_3-0) Frank, Adam (31 December 2020). [\"A new frontier is opening in the search for extraterrestrial life - The reason we haven't found life elsewhere in the universe is simple: We haven't really looked until now\"](https://www.washingtonpost.com/outlook/2020/12/31/breakthrough-listen-seti-technosignatures/). *[The Washington Post](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Washington_Post \"The Washington Post\")*. Retrieved 1 January 2021. 4. [1](#cite_ref-almar-201