\nOwens Valley Radio Observatory, California Institute of Technology, Big Pine, CA 93513, USADavid Woody\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nOwens Valley Radio Observatory, California Institute of Technology, Big Pine, CA 93513, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1734-2472](https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1734-2472)Sijie Yu\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nCenter for Solar-Terrestrial Research, New Jersey Institute of Technology, Newark, NJ 07102, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2872-2614](https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2872-2614)Thomas Zentmeyer\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nOwens Valley Radio Observatory, California Institute of Technology, Big Pine, CA 93513, USAPeijin Zhang(张沛锦)\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nCenter for Solar-Terrestrial Research, New Jersey Institute of Technology, Newark, NJ 07102, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6855-5799](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6855-5799)T. Joseph W. Lazio\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nDepartment of Climate and Space Sciences and Engineering, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0009-0003-8984-388X](https://orcid.org/0009-0003-8984-388X)\n\n## Article metrics\n\n**338**Total downloads\n**0**Video abstract views\n\n## Share this article\n- [](mailto:?subject=All-sky Ultra-narrowband Spectral Imaging with the OVRO–LWA: Technosignature Constraints and Axion-like Particle Prospects&body=All-sky Ultra-narrowband Spectral Imaging with the OVRO–LWA: Technosignature Constraints and Axion-like Particle Prospects - https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0)\n- [](https://www.linkedin.com/sharing/share-offsite/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiopscience.iop.org%2Farticle%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fae77f0)\n- [](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fae77f0)\n- [](https://x.com/share?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fae77f0&text=All-sky+Ultra-narrowband+Spectral+Imaging+with+the+OVRO%E2%80%93LWA%3A+Technosignature+Constraints+and+Axion-like+Particle+Prospects&via=AAS_Publishing)\n- [](https://www.mendeley.com/import/?doi=10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fae77f0)\n- [](https://bsky.app/intent/compose?text=All-sky+Ultra-narrowband+Spectral+Imaging+with+the+OVRO%E2%80%93LWA%3A+Technosignature+Constraints+and+Axion-like+Particle+Prospects%0Ahttps%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fae77f0%0Avia+%40ioppublishing.bsky.social)\n\n## \n\n## Dates\n- Received2026 April 20\n- Revised2026 May 29\n- Accepted2026 June 2\n- Published2026 June 24\n\n[](https://crossmark.crossref.org/dialog/?doi=10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0&domain=pdf)\n\nUnified Astronomy Thesaurus concepts\n\n[Dark matter](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/353);[Radio astronomy](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/1338);[Search for extraterrestrial intelligence](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/2127);[Technosignatures](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/2128)\n\n[Journal RSS](https://iopscience.iop.org/journal/rss/1538-3881)\n\n[Create or edit your corridor alerts](https://myiopscience.iop.org/signin?origin=a0&idhub=true&return=https%3A%2F%2Fiopscience.iop.org%2Farticle%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fae77f0)\n\n[What are corridors?](https://journals.aas.org/topical-corridors/)\n\n1538-3881/172/1/44\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present an imaging-domain search for technosignatures at decametric wavelengths with the Owens Valley Radio Observatory Long Wavelength Array, targeting ultra-narrowband continuous-wave signals between 50 and 86 MHz. We implement an offline graphics processing unit pipeline that processes raw voltage data with upchannelization to approximately 10 Hz frequency resolution, producing all-sky images for each fine channel and totaling more than 3 × 106images for a single 30 s epoch. Candidate selection is performed using multikernel matched filtering across frequencies, empirical noise standardization, and false-discovery-rate control. After applying quality cuts that remove extended sources, corrupted images, and obvious radio frequency interference, three narrowband candidates with signal-to-noise ratios above 10*σ*were selected for detailed analysis. By reimaging these candidates with finer temporal and spectral resolution, we resolved their structure and found them to be inconsistent with compact celestial narrowband emitters. Consequently, we report no detection of extraterrestrial technosignatures. The representative sensitivity of the search is ∼100 Jy per channel across the entire visible hemisphere. For an unresolved emitter, this corresponds to 10*σ*equivalent isotropic radiated power limits of about 1014W at a distance of 10 pc and 1018W at 1 kpc. The wide field of view and ultrafine spectral resolution of this approach enable simultaneous probing of technosignature signals from millions of stellar systems. This method further establishes a scalable framework for deeper integrations and stacked searches toward neutron star targets relevant to axion-like particle line conversion.\n\nExport citation and abstract[BibTeX](https://iopscience.iop.org/export?type=article&doi=10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0&exportFormat=iopexport_bib&exportType=abs&navsubmit=Export+abstract)[RIS](https://iopscience.iop.org/export?type=article&doi=10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0&exportFormat=iopexport_ris&exportType=abs&navsubmit=Export+abstract)\n\n[**Previous**article in issue](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae7a48)\n\n[**Next**article in issue](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae75ad)\n\n## \n\n- [NASA ADS Record](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2026AJ....172...44K)\n- [About Related Links](https://iopscience.iop.org/journal/1538-3881/page/About%20Related%20Links)\n\nOriginal content from this work may be used under the terms of the[Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe search for radio technosignatures—the engineered emissions of technological civilizations—remains one of the most mature and observationally tractable approaches to addressing the question “Are we alone in the Universe?” Conducted across meter-to-centimeter wavelengths, these experiments exploit radio astronomy’s fine spectral resolution, wide instantaneous bandwidths, and the transparency of the interstellar medium over much of the radio band. Foundational arguments for interstellar radio communication and the “watering hole” near the Hiline framed the early searches (e.g., G. Cocconi & P. Morrison[1959](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib17); F. D. Drake[1961](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib25); J. Tarter[2001](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib78); NASA Technosignatures Workshop Participants[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib60); J. T. Wright et al.[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib97); D. Li et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib50)). Modern programs—e.g., Breakthrough Listen’s multi-GHz campaigns with the Robert C. Byrd Green Bank Telescope (GBT), Murriyang (Parkes Radio Telescope), the Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical Telescope (FAST), and the Meer Karoo Array Telescope (MeerKAT) pathfinders—refine drift-search pipelines and implement rigorous on/off, multibeam discrimination (e.g., J. E. Enriquez et al.[2017](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib29); S. P. Worden et al.[2017](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib96); D. C. Price et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib67); D. Czech et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib23); S. Z. Sheikh[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib75); J.-L. Margot et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib53); B. Brzycki et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib9); P. X. Ma et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib52)) while extending coverage across increasingly broad portions of the radio spectrum, though still predominantly at decimeter-to-centimeter wavelengths (∼0.6–8 GHz).\n\nIn recent years, a growing fraction of the search space (S. Z. Sheikh[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib74)) has opened at low radio frequencies (≲300 MHz). These bands, historically underexplored relative to the GHz regime, offer unique advantages: lower-cost high equivalent isotropic radiated power (EIRP) beacons, leakage analogs of terrestrial broadcast/aviation/maritime services, enormous instantaneous fields of view, and excellent survey speed with sparse-aperture arrays. Recent dual-station Low-Frequency Array (LOFAR) experiments have demonstrated the effectiveness of simultaneous multisite anticoincidence radio frequency interference (RFI) rejection across ∼106TESS/Gaia targets in the 110–190 MHz band (O. A. Johnson et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib45)), extending earlier anticoincidence strategies developed in radio SETI (W. J. Welch[1992](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib94); G. R. Harp[2005](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib37); G. R. Harp et al.[2016](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib38)) into the low-frequency regime.\n\nLow-frequency ultrafine spectral imaging is also directly applicable to ultralight dark matter searches. Axions arise as pseudo-Nambu–Goldstone bosons from the Peccei–Quinn solution to the strong-charge-parity problem, while more general axion-like particles (ALPs) share the two-photon coupling but are not tied to quantum chromodynamics (QCD); both can mix with photons in magnetized plasmas (e.g., P. W. Graham et al.[2015](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib34); D. J. E. Marsh[2016](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib54); I. G. Irastorza & J. Redondo[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib44); A. J. Millar et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib56); F. Chadha-Day et al.[2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib13)). In neutron star magnetospheres, axion/ALP dark matter can resonantly convert into narrow radio lines where the local plasma frequency matches the particle mass, yielding an observed line near\n\nwhere*ν*is the photon frequency of the converted radio line and*m**a*is the axion/ALP mass expressed in energy units. This motivates targeted radio searches toward magnetars and isolated neutron stars (e.g., A. Hook et al.[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib40); B. R. Safdi et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib73); M. Leroy et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib49); R. A. Battye et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib5); S. J. Witte et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib95)). The intrinsic line width is primarily set by the dark matter velocity dispersion, typically Δ*ν*/*ν*∼ 10−6, placing such signals well within the regime probed by ultranarrow spectral channels.\n\nDetectability at tens of megahertz is strongly model-dependent: at these low frequencies, the resonant layer lies farther from the neutron star, where magnetic fields are weaker and propagation effects can diminish the escaping flux. Even so, wide-field low-frequency arrays remain compelling for ALPs with enhanced photon couplings and for population-level searches that stack many neutron stars statistically. Analogous conversion processes have also been explored for other light bosons, such as dark photons, with recent LOFAR observations of the solar corona between ∼30 and 80 MHz placing new constraints on dark-photon mixing (H. An et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib1)).\n\n'Lastly, a complementary channel is two-photon decay*a*→*γγ*: while spontaneous decay is negligible for*μ*eV axions, a bright ambient radio field can yield large stimulated emission, motivating diffuse narrow-line searches toward bright backgrounds (e.g., the inner Galaxy) (A. Caputo et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib10); P. S. Bhupal Dev et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib8)).\n\nBoth science cases therefore motivate instruments that combine ultrafine spectral resolution with wide-field radio imaging that can be implemented efficiently at large data volumes. Meeting these requirements increasingly relies on software-defined interferometers, in which digital channelization, correlation or beamforming, RFI excision, and imaging are implemented on graphics processing unit (GPU) clusters. Examples include LOFAR and its ongoing LOFAR2.0 upgrade, which combines distributed clock synchronization with software-based correlation and beamforming running on large compute clusters, enabling simultaneous low- and high-band operation and flexible calibration and survey modes (M. P. van Haarlem et al.[2013](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib90); H. W. Edler et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib28); B. Hut et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib42)). The Murchison Widefield Array (MWA) Phase II combined a reconfigurable layout with a GPU-based correlator and real-time calibration and imaging pipeline, improving*uv*-coverage, angular resolution, and wide-field imaging sensitivity (R. B. Wayth et al.[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib93)). On a general-purpose dish array, the Karl G. Jansky Very Large Array (VLA) has been equipped with the Commensal Open-Source Multimode Interferometer Cluster (COSMIC) backend, a GPU-based pipeline running during routine operations for technosignature and narrow-line searches (C. D. Tremblay et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib88)). One of the upcoming facilities, the Deep Synoptic Array (DSA), is explicitly designed as a radio camera, producing science-ready images nearly in real time and exemplifying the software-telescope paradigm (G. Hallinan et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib36),[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib35)). Within this evolving landscape, the Owens Valley Radio Observatory Long Wavelength Array (OVRO–LWA) (G. Hallinan et al. 2026, in preparation) brings these capabilities to the 13–86 MHz regime, enabling all-sky, high-cadence imaging ideally suited for broadband transient and narrowband line searches while also serving as a pathfinder for DSA.\n\nThe OVRO–LWA is a low-frequency interferometer located at Owens Valley Radio Observatory, California (37.24∘N, 118.28∘W). While using the same basic dipole design as the New Mexico LWA stations (e.g., G. B. Taylor et al.[2012](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib79)), it was independently engineered for all-sky, arcminute-scale imaging, requiring extensive custom hardware. The array was recently expanded to 352 dual-polarization antennas: 241 in a ∼200 m core and 111 in an extended layout with baselines up to 2.4 km. The system covers 13–86.5 MHz with native 24 kHz channels, cross-correlating all antennas to deliver ∼20,000 deg2wide-field images at resolutions from ∼5′ to 30′ across the band. The array facilitates a diverse range of science cases, covering the search for exoplanetary magnetospheres and the tracking of stellar space weather (e.g., I. Davis et al.[2025](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib24)), as well as probing the cosmic dawn via the 21 cm hydrogen line (e.g., M. W. Eastwood et al.[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib26),[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib27); H. Garsden et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib33)). Further capabilities include dynamic spectroscopic imaging of solar phenomena (e.g., S. Chhabra et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib15); X. Chen et al.[2025](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib14); S. Mondal et al.[2025a](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib57),[2025b](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib58); P. Zhang et al.[2025](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib99),[2026](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib98)), measuring cosmic-ray air showers (e.g., R. Monroe et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib59); K. Plant et al.[2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib65); K. A. Plant et al.[2026](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib66)), monitoring for low-frequency transient events (e.g., M. M. Anderson et al.[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib2),[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib3)), analyzing radio afterglows from meteor trails (e.g., S. S. Varghese et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib92)), and studying galaxy clusters (e.g., K.-C. Hou et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib41)).\n\nMore recently, a system was developed at the OVRO–LWA specifically for fast-transient searches of prompt radio emission from gravitational-wave (GW) events, with its full design, validation, and application to a real GW event described in N. Kosogorov et al. ([2025](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib46),[2026](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib47)). This system introduced upchannelized beamforming and imaging, allowing for extremely narrow frequency channels of about 700 Hz. In this work, we build on this capability for ultra-narrow-channel imaging at about 10 Hz resolution.\n\nIn this paper, we explore how the OVRO–LWA can be applied to searches for technosignatures and ALPs. In Section[2](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s2), we present the observations, data processing, propagation, and kinematic considerations relevant to narrowband signals and the procedures used for candidate identification, vetting, and higher-resolution follow-up. In Section[3](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s3), we translate the search into technosignature constraints and discuss the implications for ALP searches. Section[4](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s4)summarizes the main results and future directions.\n\n## 2. Observations, Data Processing, and Signal Detectability\n\nThis section describes the OVRO–LWA observations and data processing pipelines used to produce ultra-narrowband all-sky images, followed by a summary of propagation and kinematic effects that inform the detectability and interpretation of narrowband signals and the subsequent procedures for candidate detection and validation. Figure[1](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0f1)provides an overview of the full processing pipeline used in this work.\n\n**Figure 1.**Summary of the OVRO–LWA ultra-narrowband imaging search pipeline. Raw voltage data are upchannelized and cross-correlated (Section[2.1](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s2-1)), calibrated, and imaged (Section[2.2](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s2-2)) into all-sky 10 Hz, 30 s image cubes. These are then normalized and searched for candidates (Section[2.4](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s2-4)), followed by vetting and higher-resolution analysis (Sections[2.5](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s2-5)and[2.6](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s2-6)). The results are presented in Section[3](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0s3).\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/172/1/44/revision1/ajae77f0f1_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/172/1/44/revision1/ajae77f0f1_hr.jpg)\n\n### 2.1. Cross-correlation Pipeline\n\nFor a detailed description of the OVRO–LWA data buffer dump and offline cross-correlation pipeline, see N. Kosogorov et al. ([2025](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib46)). Below, we summarize the main components relevant to our analysis and the modifications implemented for this work.\n\nWe first record the raw output of the OVRO–LWA F-engine after polyphase filter bank (PFB) processing across the 50–86 MHz band and dump it to the GPU servers for initial storage. These data are then transferred to our 5 PB storage system for further processing. We then use the offline cross-correlation pipeline, which upchannelizes the data by applying a fast Fourier transform (FFT) to the time samples within each coarse channel, thereby increasing the spectral resolution. For fast-transient searches associated with GW events, the typical upchannelization factor is 32. Using the default online channelization of about 23.9 kHz, this results in channels of about 750 Hz. In our case, however, we choose a factor of 2400, resulting in a final channel resolution of 9.9 Hz. Given the large data volume produced by upchannelization, we divide the data into manageable chunks of 300 fine channels, corresponding to approximately 3 kHz of bandwidth per batch. For each batch, the selected fine channels are cross-correlated on a GPU, and the procedure is then repeated for the next batch of 300 fine channels until the full 50–86 MHz band is covered. Independent batches can be distributed across the available GPUs, allowing multiple portions of the band to be processed in parallel while keeping the memory footprint of each GPU job manageable.\n\nCross-correlation is implemented within the Bifrost framework (M. D. Cranmer et al.[2017](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib22)) and executed on available GPUs using dedicated processing blocks.[15](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0fn1)The correlation stage is accelerated by a GPU kernel built on the xGPU library[16](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0fn2)(M. A. Clark et al.[2011](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib16)). The resulting visibilities are stored in hierarchical data format (HDF) files with an integration time of 30 s. Subsequently, the visibilities are converted into the Common Astronomy Software Applications (CASA)[17](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0fn3)(CASA Team et al.[2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib11)) measurement set (MS) format while retaining all necessary metadata.\n\nThe dataset analyzed in this work was obtained on 2024 April 6 at 06:30:57 UTC and consists of a single 30 s observing epoch used for the primary search. For candidate follow-up, we additionally reprocessed subsequent 30 s integrations spanning approximately 3 minutes. Although the Galactic plane dominates the sky temperature at tens of MHz, its emission is spectrally smooth. Consequently, while the Galaxy contributes to the overall system noise, it does not mimic the spectral characteristics of ultra-narrowband features. We did not impose any constraint on the Galactic-plane elevation, as our search algorithm effectively separates this broadband continuum from narrow spectral lines. The current configuration of 10 Hz channelization and 30 s integration is motivated by the need to probe fractional line widths of order Δ*ν*/*ν*∼ 10−6at tens of MHz while remaining computationally feasible. For this analysis, we utilized eight NVIDIA RTX A4000 GPUs to perform the offline cross-correlation.\n\n### 2.2. Calibration and Imaging Pipeline\n\nWe then proceeded with calibrating the resulting visibility dataset. For gain calibration, we adopted a simplified sky model consisting of flux densities and spectral indices for the brightest radio sources (J. W. M. Baars et al.[1977](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib4); R. A. Perley & B. J. Butler[2017](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib63)). At the time of observation, the only sufficiently bright calibrator above the horizon was Virgo A. The same MSs were then used to calibrate each channel individually, as the calibrator was bright enough for this purpose.\n\nThe calibration included a bandpass correction using thebandpasstask within CASA[18](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0fn4)(CASA Team et al.[2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib11)). Imaging was performed withWSClean[19](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0fn5)(A. R. Offringa et al.[2014](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib62)) over the visible hemisphere on a 512 × 512 grid.\n\nWe show the online cross-correlated data image in Figure[2](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0f2)for the same local sidereal time (LST), and in Figure[3](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0f3), we present an example 10 Hz, 30 s integration image of the full sky. The wideband reference image shown in Figure[2](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0f2)was made with a finer imaging grid, whereas the ultra-narrowband search images, including Figure[3](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0f3), were produced on the coarser 512 × 512 grid with 025 pixels. This coarser grid was adopted to make the production and storage of more than 3 × 106images tractable, and the larger restoring beam in Figure[3](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0f3)reflects the effective resolution of this search-image product. These images are retained as the sole data products for subsequent analysis. The 10 Hz, 30 s image has a sensitivity of about 120 Jy (1*σ*). The Virgo A detection shows a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of ∼19, consistent with the expected value of ∼20, indicating that the image noise and flux calibration are within expected limits.\n\n**Figure 2.**All-sky OVRO–LWA map in J2000 equatorial coordinates, generated from real-time cross-correlation data for a single 10 s integration across the 50–86 MHz band. R.A. is shown in hours and decl. in degrees. The synthesized beam is 5494 × 4537 (FWHM) at a position angle of 449.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/172/1/44/revision1/ajae77f0f2_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/172/1/44/revision1/ajae77f0f2_hr.jpg)\n\n**Figure 3.**All-sky OVRO–LWA ultra-narrowband (∼10 Hz) map in J2000 equatorial coordinates, generated from the offline cross-correlation pipeline for a 30 s integration at 72,675,248 Hz. R.A. is shown in hours and decl. in degrees. The synthesized beam is(FWHM) at a position angle of 1255. Because this image corresponds to a single 10 Hz channel, its noise is much higher than in the wideband image, and most broadband continuum emission and most point sources are not significantly detected. Vir A, near R.A. =12h30mand decl. = +124, is clearly visible; the white plus sign marks its position.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/172/1/44/revision1/ajae77f0f3_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/172/1/44/revision1/ajae77f0f3_hr.jpg)\n\n### 2.3. Propagation and Kinematic Effects\n\nBefore introducing candidate selection, we summarize the propagation and kinematic effects that shape intrinsically narrow tones in our images. These effects determine the apparent width, amplitude modulation, and detectability of any true narrowband emitter.\n\n#### 2.3.1. Dispersion and Scintillation\n\nCold plasma dispersion introduces a deterministic, frequency-dependent group delay that affects all signals propagating through the interstellar medium. For a steady, continuous narrowband tone, this delay is constant in time and therefore has no observable effect on detectability. Within a 10 Hz channel at our observing frequencies, the intrachannel delay predicted by the standard dispersion relation is below the millisecond level for dispersion measure (DM) ∼ 1–10 pc cm−3, which is negligible compared to our 30 s integrations. Dispersion is thus only relevant for transient signals on subsecond timescales.\n\nTo assess diffractive interstellar scintillation (DISS), we convert empirical pulse-broadening timescales to equivalent diffractive decorrelation bandwidths using standard thin-screen relations. We adopt the pulse-broadening–DM scalings, together with their large intrinsic sight-line-to-sight-line scatter, as characterized by both N. D. R. Bhat et al. ([2004](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib7)) and the updated analysis of J. M. Cordes et al. ([2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib20)). At our observing frequencies and DM of ∼a few tens of pc cm−3, the typical diffractive bandwidths are tens of Hz to kHz, while the observed scatter allows substantially smaller values, extending to ≲1 Hz along strongly scattered lines of sight. Consequently, the number of independent scintles across a 10 Hz channel can range from order unity to ≫1, depending on the line of sight.\n\nThe DISS timescale, set by the drift of the diffraction pattern across the line of sight (and hence by the effective transverse velocity), is typically of order minutes at ∼GHz frequencies (e.g., B. J. Rickett[1990](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib71); J. M. Cordes & B. J. Rickett[1998](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib21); D. J. Reardon et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib68)). For a Kolmogorov medium, the diffractive scintillation timescale scales with observing frequency as Δ*t*d∝*ν*6/5(e.g., M. T. Lam et al.[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib48)). Applying this scaling to meter wavelengths implies characteristic DISS timescales of order seconds to tens of seconds across our observing band, with ∼10–30 s as a representative value. Over a 30 s integration, this corresponds to sampling roughly one to a few independent scintles in time, such that the resulting modulation index can range from near unity down to a few tenths, depending on the line of sight.\n\nIn summary, the total intensity modulation can vary widely, from about 100% down to below 10%, depending on both the diffractive bandwidth and the temporal scintillation rate along a given sight line.\n\n#### 2.3.2. Free–Free Absorption and Angular Broadening\n\nWe estimate thermal free–free optical depth using the P. G. Mezger & A. P. Henderson ([1967](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib55)) approximation with a representative electron temperature of about 8000 K. At a representative frequency of 70 MHz, this yields optical depth*τ*≈ 1.2 × 10−4EM/(pc cm−6), where EM is the emission measure, with only factor-of-unity variation across the 50–86 MHz range. For smooth, low-DM paths off the Galactic plane, the implied EMs are small and*τ*≪ 10−3. Even for much denser, clumpier paths with EM ∼ 100 pc cm−6, attenuation remains at the percent level. We therefore neglect free–free absorption for most sight lines considered here.\n\nFor a Kolmogorov spectrum of density fluctuations, image broadening steeply increases toward lower frequency. Empirically, off-plane sight lines at these frequencies show subarcsecond broadening, while generic paths in the plane show arcsecond-scale values. Only the strongest inner Galaxy directions, such as toward the Galactic center, reach arcminute scales at these frequencies. With a 5′–10′ synthesized beam, angular broadening is negligible for the vast majority of the sky and only potentially comparable along the most strongly scattered lines of sight.\n\n#### 2.3.3. Spectral Broadening from Intervening Plasma\n\nPropagation through circumstellar or interplanetary plasma may broaden an intrinsically ultranarrow technosignature, especially at low observing frequencies and for lines of sight passing close to the host star; transient activity such as coronal mass ejections may enhance this effect further (V. Gajjar & G. C. Brown[2026](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib32)). Interstellar scattering can likewise broaden intrinsically narrow signals and, in principle, may impose an effective horizon once the broadened width becomes comparable to or larger than the search channel width (e.g., J. M. Cordes & T. J. Lazio[1991](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib18); J. M. Cordes et al.[1997](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib19)). Such broadening is expected to be more pronounced at low observing frequencies and along strongly scattered sight lines (e.g., C. D. Tremblay et al.[2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib84)). We do not model this source by source here, but it motivates searching not only for single-channel excesses but also for modestly broadened features. A more detailed sight-line-dependent treatment using modern Galactic electron-density models, such as NE2025, is left for future work (S. K. Ocker & J. M. Cordes[2026](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib61)).\n\n#### 2.3.4. Doppler Drift\n\nDoppler drift is a kinematic effect arising from the relative acceleration of the transmitter and receiver. For the receiver alone (Earth’s rotation and orbital motion), the expected drift at 70 MHz over a 30 s integration is small compared to our 10 Hz channel width. Scaling the standard narrowband SETI estimates for Earth-induced drift rates to 70 MHz gives a maximum receiver-induced drift of only a few tenths of a Hz over 30 s, even in the most unfavorable line-of-sight case (S. Z. Sheikh et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib77); D. C. Price et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib67)). Thus, for most signals, the power remains concentrated in the same 10 Hz channel during a single integration.\n\nA signal that begins very near a channel boundary can still drift into an adjacent bin, but for receiver-induced drift alone, this should affect only a small minority of cases, typically at the few-percent level. In this sense, Doppler drift from Earth’s motion is usually a minor correction for our present 30 s, 10 Hz analysis.\n\nFor transmitters bound to planets, rotational and orbital accelerations can dominate the observed drift (S. Z. Sheikh et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib77)). In particular, close-in giant planets around Sun-like stars can plausibly produce total drifts of tens of Hz over 30 s, depending on geometry, while tidally locked rotation typically contributes a smaller but still nonnegligible term. Our current search does not apply drift-rate corrections; future implementations can include a modest drift search spanning at least tens of Hz over 30 s to capture the most extreme close-in planetary cases (S. Z. Sheikh et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib77)).\n\n### 2.4. Candidate Identification Pipeline\n\nWe use a two-stage procedure to identify and initially vet candidates. In*Stage I*, we search the standardized image cube for statistically significant narrowband outliers over a range of assumed line widths. In*Stage II*, we reject duplicate detections arising from adjacent frequency bins or multiple trial line widths at the same sky position, along with corrupted images and candidates inconsistent with unresolved narrowband point sources.\n\nWe first standardize each channelized all-sky image by estimating the median absolute deviation (MAD) and converting the result to maps with approximately unit variance. To remove broad spectral structure, we subtract a running median along the frequency axis at each sky pixel, using a window much wider (over 5 kHz) than the widest lines in our search (about 1 kHz). This high-pass step preserves narrowband features while suppressing continuum emission, bandpass ripples, and other slowly varying spectral trends.\n\nTo accommodate uncertain intrinsic line widths, we convolve each per-pixel frequency series with a set of unit-normalized boxcar kernels spanning widths from 1 to 100 fine channels. For our ≃10 Hz channel spacing, this corresponds to line widths from ∼10 Hz to ∼1 kHz. The kernel widths are sampled densely at the narrowest line widths, with single-channel (≃10 Hz) spacing up to around ∼100 Hz, followed by progressively coarser spacing at broader line widths: steps of a few fine channels over ∼100–500 Hz and tens of fine channels near the upper end of the ∼1 kHz range. This width-aware search is also motivated by the possibility that intrinsically ultranarrow extraterrestrial signals may be spectrally broadened during propagation through circumstellar plasma, especially at low radio frequencies (V. Gajjar & G. C. Brown[2026](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae77f0#ajae77f0bib32)). For every pixel and channel, we retain the highest SNR and record the corresponding e", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:23:35.818899+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:75b64f750ec11ec7c6129685", "attempt_id": "attempt:807d733238d21d47e53fad6c", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c"}, "result": "## Detecting Technosignatures from Earth-scale Civilizations - IOPscience\n\n**Source**: https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c\n\n---\n\n[](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c)\n\n[](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/journal/0004-637X)\n\n[The American Astronomical Society (AAS)](https://aas.org/), established in 1899 and based in Washington, DC, is the major organization of professional astronomers in North America. Its membership of about 7,000 individuals also includes physicists, mathematicians, geologists, engineers, and others whose research and educational interests lie within the broad spectrum of subjects comprising contemporary astronomy. The mission of the AAS is to enhance and share humanity's scientific understanding of the universe.\n\nThe following article isOpen access\n\n# Detecting Technosignatures from Earth-scale Civilizations\n\nReza Ashtari\n\nPublished 2023 October 20•© 2023. The Author(s). Published by the American Astronomical Society.\n[The Astrophysical Journal](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/journal/0004-637X),[Volume 957](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/volume/0004-637X/957),[Number 1](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/issue/0004-637X/957/1)**Citation**Reza Ashtari 2023*ApJ***957**15**DOI**10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c\n\n[PDFOpens in a new tab.](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c/pdf)\n\n[ePub](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c/epub)\n\nYou need an eReader or compatible software to experience[the benefits of the ePub3 file format](http://iopscience.iop.org/page/ePub3).\n\n## \n\nReza Ashtari\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nThe Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory, 11100 Johns Hopkins Road, Laurel, MD 20723, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8943-9148](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8943-9148)\n\n## Article metrics\n\n**2889**Total downloads\n**0**Video abstract views\n\n## Share this article\n- [](mailto:?subject=Detecting Technosignatures from Earth-scale Civilizations&body=Detecting Technosignatures from Earth-scale Civilizations - https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c)\n- [](https://www.linkedin.com/sharing/share-offsite/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbeta.iopscience.iop.org%2Farticle%2F10.3847%2F1538-4357%2Facf56c)\n- [](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-4357%2Facf56c)\n- [](https://x.com/share?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-4357%2Facf56c&text=Detecting+Technosignatures+from+Earth-scale+Civilizations&via=AAS_Publishing)\n- [](https://www.mendeley.com/import/?doi=10.3847%2F1538-4357%2Facf56c)\n- [](https://bsky.app/intent/compose?text=Detecting+Technosignatures+from+Earth-scale+Civilizations%0Ahttps%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-4357%2Facf56c%0Avia+%40ioppublishing.bsky.social)\n\n## \n\n## Dates\n- Received2023 March 13\n- Revised2023 August 28\n- Accepted2023 August 29\n- Published2023 October 20\n\n[](https://crossmark.crossref.org/dialog/?doi=10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c&domain=pdf)\n\nUnified Astronomy Thesaurus concepts\n\n[Exoplanets](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/498);[Technosignatures](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/2128);[Radio astronomy](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/1338)\n\n[Journal RSS](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/journal/rss/0004-637X)\n\n[Create or edit your corridor alerts](https://myiopscience.iop.org/signin?origin=a0&idhub=true&return=https%3A%2F%2Fbeta.iopscience.iop.org%2Farticle%2F10.3847%2F1538-4357%2Facf56c)\n\n[What are corridors?](https://journals.aas.org/topical-corridors/)\n\n0004-637X/957/1/15\n\n## Abstract\n\nAssuming civilizations have similar interplanetary communications and radar capability to NASA’s Deep Space Network, what is the feasibility of intercepting their communications? Interplanetary conjunctions between Earth-like exoplanets, their stars, and other planets in their systems provide one of the most unique and pragmatic opportunities for detecting technosignatures. While eavesdropping on terrestrial communications becomes limited by a planet’s rotation, the beams of satellite communications and interplanetary radar transmissions are tracked, providing the most persistent and powerful opportunity for signal interception. In this study, we present a framework for assessing exoplanet habitability and establishing quantitative bounds for detecting Earth-scale technosignatures from Earth-like planets. These constraints for time, frequency, sky positions, and observatory sensitivity provide recommended observational guidelines for using state-of-the-art and future ground-based radio observatories toward technosignature detection. Applying this framework, 16 exoplanet targets are proposed for radio observation.\n\nExport citation and abstract[BibTeX](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/export?type=article&doi=10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c&exportFormat=iopexport_bib&exportType=abs&navsubmit=Export+abstract)[RIS](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/export?type=article&doi=10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c&exportFormat=iopexport_ris&exportType=abs&navsubmit=Export+abstract)\n\n[**Previous**article in issue](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf0c2)\n\n[**Next**article in issue](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ace046)\n\n## \n\n- [NASA ADS Record](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2023ApJ...957...15A)\n- [Simbad Objects](https://simbad.u-strasbg.fr/simbad/sim-ref?querymethod=bib&simbo=on&bibcode=2023ApJ...957...15A)\n- [About Related Links](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/journal/0004-637X/page/About%20Related%20Links)\n\nOriginal content from this work may be used under the terms of the[Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence](http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCampaigning for astronomical observations with a topic as broad as technosignatures requires justified constraints. Using NASA’s Deep Space Network (DSN) as a benchmark for capability in deep space communications and interplanetary radar, we develop range limits for exoplanet-based technosignatures from Earth-scale civilizations for current and future radio observatories.\n\nThe most predominant framework for categorizing the technological advancement of a civilization is referred to as the Kardashev scale (Kardashev[1964](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib13)). This scale ranks different types of civilizations based on their energy usage. A Type 0 civilization is characterized by an energy consumption dependent primarily on the planet’s resources, such as fossil fuels and other nonrenewable resources (Kardashev[1964](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib13); Sagan[2000](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib28)). A Type 1 civilization, also known as a planetary civilization, is capable of using and controlling all available energy on its planet, including geothermal, wind, solar, and other renewable sources. This civilization would also have control over all natural forces, like weather and earthquakes (Kardashev[1964](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib13)). A Type 2 civilization, also referred to as a stellar civilization, could harness the total energy of its star, most likely through something like a Dyson sphere (Dyson[1960](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib9)), allowing for activities such as power consumption and communication on a stellar scale (Kardashev[1964](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib13)). Finally, a Type 3 civilization, or a galactic civilization, would be able to utilize the energy from all the stars within its galaxy, indicating a level of technological and scientific advancement far beyond our current understanding (Kardashev[1964](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib13)).\n\nEarth-scale civilizations are currently considered to be about a Type 0.7 on the Kardashev scale (Sagan[2000](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib28)). For the scope of this paper, we present the most pragmatic methods for detecting technological signatures of life from these extrasolar civilizations with technological capability comparable to Earth’s.\n\nSince we detect most exoplanets via the transit method (see Figure[1](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf1)), Earth benefits from several celestial events especially opportunistic for technosignature observation.\n\n**Figure 1.**Cumulative number of exoplanets discovered for all discovery methods vs. year (Courtesy of NASA Exoplanet Archive[2022](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib24)).\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf1_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf1_hr.jpg)\n\nObserving transiting exoplanets in the radio is not new to the search for technosignatures, with Sheikh et al. ([2023](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib32)) most recently observing the transits of 12 Kepler exoplanets using the Green Bank Telescope (GBT). However, contrary to observing the transits of such exoplanets, the framework presented in this paper justifies why the interplanetary communications and radio activity of Earth-scale civilizations will only be visible during secondary eclipses (i.e., superior conjunctions) and planet–planet occultations (PPOs), unless intentionally directed at Earth. Secondary eclipses and PPOs of transiting exoplanets present nearly ideal observational windows for measuring interplanetary communications and radar from confirmed Earth-like exoplanets. The geometries for these observational scenarios are illustrated in Figures[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf2)and[3](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf3).\n\n**Figure 2.**Geometry for observing interplanetary communications to a stellar orbiter before and after secondary eclipses.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf2_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf2_hr.jpg)\n\n**Figure 3.**Geometry for observing interplanetary communications to a planetary orbiter before and after interplanetary conjunctions. Note this configuration also applies for observing interplanetary radar.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf3_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf3_hr.jpg)\n\nComplementary to technosignatures, these radio observations also allow for measurement of stellar flares and stellar flare echoes from exoplanets (Schneider[2005](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib30)), as well as exoplanetary radio emission (Ashtari et al.[2022](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib2)). For planets orbiting M dwarfs, the impact of the flare rate on atmospheric and magnetospheric retention is a huge debate in the discussion of exoplanet habitability (Schulze-Makuch et al.[2011](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib31)). The opportunity to observe these exoplanet occultations with properly constrained observational configurations can provide an immensely bountiful step toward technosignature searches while simultaneously contributing to exoplanet science and astrophysics.\n\nQuantizing the cosmic footprint of a civilization has been templated by the concept of an “ichnoscale” in the past (Socas-Navarro et al.[2021](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib36)), where the term “ichnology” refers to the study of tracks and traces (Pemberton et al.[1992](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib26)). Rather than focusing on the technosignature footprints of Kardashev Types 1–3 civilizations, this work pragmatically frames a search for Earth-scale technosignature footprints based on Earth’s deep space communications and interplanetary radar capabilities (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19); NASA[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib23)).\n\nTargeting the spacecraft transmissions of interstellar spacecraft like Voyager, Pioneer, and New Horizons has been discussed in previous works (Derrick & Isaacson[2023](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib7)). This work limits the search for interstellar communications to based on Earth’s current transmission and reception capabilities. Hence, the only deep space or interplanetary communications we can currently detect are limited to uplink transmissions to spacecraft. The detectability of these Earth-scale technosignature footprints is detailed further in Section[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cs2).\n\n### 1.1. Stellar Orbiters: A Universal Ambition and the Case for Observing Secondary Eclipses\n\nWhile some planetary systems have multiple exoplanets, all known habitable exoplanets orbit a star. For life on planets with a single planet in the planetary system, exploring the local star is the most obvious priority for space exploration. Solar system analogs for such stellar orbiters include NASA’s Parker Solar Probe (Kinnison et al.[2020](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib14)) and the European Space Agency’s Solar Orbiter (Müller et al.[2020](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib21)) spacecraft.\n\nFollowing this principle of native space exploration instinct, we propose observations targeting communications to stellar orbiters from Earth-like exoplanets.\n\nThe geometry for observing deep space communications to stellar-orbiting satellites is shown in Figure[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf2), illustrating the importance of measuring before, during, and after secondary eclipses.\n\nFor planning these secondary eclipse measurements, observational windows are limited to when the elevation angle is high enough and the orbit of the target exoplanet is aligned properly.\n\nAn example demonstrating the process for selecting the correct times, elevations, and orbital phases for ground-based exoplanet observations is shown in Figure[4](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf4).\n\n**Figure 4.**The approach for selecting observation windows is shown above. Top: first, the elevation angle of the target is plotted. A dashed horizontal black line is shown to display the cutoff elevation angle for observations. Twenty degrees is selected here as the elevation limit, per common practice (NASA[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib23); Braun et al.[2015](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib4)). Middle: next, the orbital phase of the targeted exoplanet is evaluated over the same time period. Here, it is assumed an orbital phase of 0 is equal to the time of transit center or when the planet is between the star and our view from Earth. For observing secondary eclipses, we want to observe when the planet is behind its star, so when the orbital phase is equal to 0.5. To observe the period before, through, and after the secondary eclipse, we designate orbital phases from 0.45 to 0.55 to be of interest. Bottom: finally, cross-referencing the periods when the elevation of the target is greater than 20° and the orbit of the targeted planet is in the correct phase, we show the observational windows meeting our criteria. Each observational period/visit is shown in a different color. Data shown in these three figures are generated using the*astroplan*Python software package (Morris et al.[2018](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib18)).\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf4_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf4_hr.jpg)\n\nUsing this methodology, all 16 exoplanets listed in Table[1](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1)are proposed for secondary eclipse observation.\n\n**Table 1.**Target Exoplanets and Parameters Relative to Earth SimilarityConservative TargetsPlanet NameESIglobalEarth MassEarth RadiusDistanceStar AgeInclination(lt-yr)(Gyr)(deg)K2-3 d0.861.542.80144189.8K2-72 e[a](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1fna)0.941.322.21217⋯89.7Kepler-1649 b[a](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1fna)0.900.991.03301⋯89.2Kepler-1649 c[a](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1fna)0.951.101.20301⋯89.3Kepler-1652 b0.861.543.188223.290.0Kepler-438 b0.901.101.464734.489.9TOI-700 d0.911.101.571021.589.8TRAPPIST-1 d[a](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1fna)0.920.770.39417.689.8TRAPPIST-1 e[a](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1fna)0.880.880.69417.689.9Optimistic TargetsK2-133 e0.811.653.66245⋯89.2K2-18 b0.812.318.92124⋯89.6K2-72 c[a](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1fna)0.861.101.66217⋯89.6Kepler-440 b0.821.874.139821.389.9Kepler-445 d0.851.212.10415⋯89.6TOI-1452 b0.802.365.121301.389.8TRAPPIST-1 f[a](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1fna)0.810.680.52417.689.7\n\n**Notes.**Star age (in billions of years) is provided for evaluating technological maturation. Star distance (in light years) is used as a key reference for analyzing if the technosignature footprint from the target exoplanet is within our detection radius. The limits for each observatory’s detection radii are provided in Table[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct2), based on the power and narrowband or wideband nature of the signal to be eavesdropped. The ESI values shown are global ESI values, calculated using Equations ([C1](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ceqn12))–([C7](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ceqn18)) (Schulze-Makuch et al.[2011](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib31)). The conservative targets listed in the upper table achieve Earth similarity scores of 0.8 for both their planetary interior and surface characteristics (ESIglobal> 0.8 and ESIsurface> 0.8 and ESIinterior> 0.8). These are the targets in the green region of Figure[11](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf11). The optimistic targets listed in the lower table still achieve global ESIs of 0.8 but with either the interior or surface of the planet achieving greater than 0.8 Earth similarity (ESIglobal> 0.8 and (ESIsurface> 0.8 or ESIinterior> 0.8)).[](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c)aOccults another high-ESI exoplanet. Candidate for PPO and secondary eclipse technosignature observation.\n\nDownload table as:[ASCII](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/0004-637X/957/1/15/suppdata/apjacf56ct1_ascii.txt?doi=10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c)[Typeset image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56ct1_lr.gif)\n\n### 1.2. The Mars Analog: So Much Interplanetary Radio Traffic and the Case for Observing PPOs\n\nSome of the most intriguing exoplanetary technosignature candidates are high–Earth Similarity Index (ESI) pairs of planets. In our solar system, the amount and longevity of interplanetary communication to Mars orbiters, landers, and rovers are significant(Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19)). It is no surprise that Mars is also the most Earth-similar body in our system with an ESI of 0.64 (Schulze-Makuch et al.[2011](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib31)).\n\nFollowing this Mars analogy, PPOs between two high-ESI exoplanets present highly favorable scenarios for detecting interplanetary communications and radar.\n\nIt should be noted the process for accurately timing PPOs is more convoluted (Luger et al.[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib16)) than observing secondary eclipses. The timing error for both planets’ ephemerides begins to compound when forecasting future PPO events, making accurately timed predictions of PPOs challenging (Luger et al.[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib16)).\n\nIn the case of eavesdropping on interplanetary technosignatures, more timing errors translates to more misalignments between the center of the technosignature’s transmission beam and Earth. This misalignment, illustrated in Figures[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf2)−[3](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf3), has a significant impact on the feasibility and bounds for our search, as discussed in Section[3.2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cs3-2).\n\nAcknowledging the difficulty associated with successfully measuring PPO events, this work proposes PPO observations for seven of the targeted exoplanets, shown in Table[1](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct1).\n\n## 2. Detectability of Technosignatures: Interplanetary Communications and Radar\n\nIf there is interplanetary communication or radar operations from a ∼Type 0.7 civilization present, an Earth-based radio telescope must target the exoplanet while the emission is beamed toward Earth (illustrated in Figures[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf2)and[3](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf3)). Unless a beamed transmission is intentionally pointed with ∼20″ precision (see Section[3.2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cs3-2)), then we need to adopt a more pragmatic framework in searching for these technosignatures. Observing the correct sky position at the right time, frequency, sensitivity, and tolerance for pointing misalignment is crucial to exoplanetary technosignature detection.\n\nAs most exoplanets are discovered via the transit method (see Figure[1](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf1)), the characteristic low orbital inclinations of these planets provide unique opportunities for technosignature detection. By observing secondary eclipses (i.e., superior conjunctions) and PPOs, we are provided an opportunistic alignment and time window for eavesdropping on the interplanetary communications and radar activity of other civilizations. These concepts for observing secondary eclipses and PPOs for technosignatures are illustrated in Figures[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf2)and[3](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf3).\n\nTargeting signals from a civilization with comparable technological ability to ours, we can begin bounding our search by limiting the ability of these civilizations to that of NASA’s DSN.\n\nThe maximum available transmission power for the DSN is offered at Deep Space Station (DSS) 43. Located in Canberra, Australia (Slobin & Pham[2013](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib35); NASA[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib23)), DSS-43 boasts a 70 m diameter antenna capable of transmitting a peak radio frequency (RF) power of 100 kW. This transmission power is adopted as the upper bound for Earth-scale deep space communications scenarios (NASA[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib23)). These signals are predominantly narrowband (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19)) in long-distance, point-to-point communications (as opposed to broadcasting communication systems) to conserve spectral power efficiency and capacity of communications links (Yuen[2013](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib41)). Deep space communications are an extreme case of directive, point-to-point communications. Thus, deep space communications are often either structured as narrowband carrier signals with minimally separated subcarriers or have no subcarriers, instead modulating the data directly onto the carrier signal (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19); Yuen[2013](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib41)).\n\nFor interplanetary radar operations, the DSN’s historical peak operating power of 1 MW, used at the 70 m antenna in Goldstone, California (DSS-14), is used as the upper bound (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19); Slobin & Pham[2013](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib35)). It is of historical significance to note that Arecibo’s larger 300 m diameter antenna offered 1 MW of transmit power before its collapse in 2021 (Venditti et al.[2023](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib40)). Taking this into account, it is worth noting the assumptions for transmitting 1 MW from a 70 m antenna are not absolute upper limits for the radio technosignature footprint of a ∼Type 0.7 civilization like Earth (Sagan[2000](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib28)). Interplanetary radar is historically operated using narrowband signals (Skolnik[1962](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib33)), as well as wideband signals (Skolnik[1962](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib33)) for performing an array of measurements including size, distance, Doppler, and spectroscopy (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19)).\n\nUsing these benchmark DSN values for the transmission capability of Earth-scale civilizations, the calculations in the[Appendix](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56capp1)are used to establish quantitative bounds toward technosignature observation.\n\nFigure[5](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf5)compares the sensitivities of current and future ground-based radio observatories against the flux densities of narrowband signals (e.g., interplanetary communications or radar from DSN-like sources 10, 100, and 1000 lt-yr away. The observatories assessed for technosignature detection in this work are the Allen Telescope Array (ATA), Australia Telescope Compact Array (ATCA), GBT, MeerKAT, and Very Large Array (VLA; Booth & Jonas[2012](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib3); Stevens et al.[2014](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib37); Maddalena et al.[2020](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib17); National Radio Astronomy Observatory[2023](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib25)), along with two future telescopes in the Square Kilometre Array (SKA) and next-generation VLA (ngVLA; Braun et al.[2015](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib4); Murphy et al.[2018](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib22)). ATA specifications were provided via W. Farrah (private communication, 2023).\n\n**Figure 5.**Detectability of exoplanetary narrowband communications (70 m antenna diameter, 100 kW of RF transmission power, 0.005°(18″) pointing accuracy). Observatory sensitivities are calculated using Equation ([B3](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ceqn11)), assuming measurement across two polarizations, and a 3 Hz receiver channel bandwidth (standard for Breakthrough Listen radio receivers; Enriquez et al.[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib10)). The takeaway of this is that for the foreseeable future, no current or planned radio observatory will be able to even detect a narrowband technosignature from greater than 100 lt-yr, unless the transmission is beyond NASA’s current deep space communication capabilities (NASA[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib23)).\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf5_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf5_hr.jpg)\n\nFigure[6](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf6)compares the sensitivities of current and future ground-based radio observatories against the flux densities of wideband signals (e.g., interplanetary radar) from sources 10, 100, and 1000 lt-yr away.\n\n**Figure 6.**Detectability of exoplanetary wideband radar signals (70 m antenna diameter, 1 MW of RF transmission power, 0.005°(18″) pointing accuracy). Observatory sensitivities are calculated using Equation ([B2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ceqn10)). Sensitivity values of 1*σ*assume measurements across two polarizations and 108samples (*τ*intΔ*v*= 108samples). As shown in Table[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct2)and illustrated in Figure[12](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf12), these high-powered wideband signals feasibly provide our observatories with greater search radii but are not as predictable, prevalent, or persistent as narrowband interplanetary communications relative to Earth’s recent history of deep space transmissions (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19)).\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf6_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf6_hr.jpg)\n\nFor both Figures[5](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf5)and[6](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf6), the flux seen from the technosignature footprint is shown for observations made from distances of 10, 100, and 1000 lt-yr (shown as blue, red, and yellow lines). The black lines and markers designate the 5*σ*sensitivity levels (defined in Equations ([B3](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ceqn11)) and ([B3](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ceqn11))) of the observatories considered.\n\nAs an example, consider the case of the red line in Figures[5](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf5)and[6](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf6), designating the flux observed at Earth from a technosignature a ∼Type 0.7 Kardashev scale civilization 100 lt-yr away.\n\nFigure[5](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf5)demonstrates for the conventionally narrowband signals used for NASA’s deep space communications (100 kW of RF power transmitted from a 70 m dish antenna), no current or future observatories will be able to successfully eavesdrop on the communications of an Earth-scale civilization within 100 lt-yr.\n\nComparatively, Figure[6](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf6)shows wideband transmissions like those used in interplanetary radar transmissions (Skolnik[1962](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib33); Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19)), using NASA’s historical peak transmit power of 1 MW (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19)), to generate higher levels of flux at Earth. Following the example for the detectability of an Earth-scale signal from 100 lt-yr, Figure[6](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf6)shows the black lines associated with all observatory sensitivities are below the red flux line, indicating that all observatories considered could detect the flux of an Earth-scale interplanetary radar transmission from a distance of 100 lt-yr (if a wideband waveform is used for operations).\n\nThe maximum search distances for each observatory are given in Table[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct2). Limits for observing with narrowband and wideband receiver configurations are provided. While more powerful wideband transmissions are more easily detected, the history of narrowband interplanetary radio communications presents a more persistent benchmark for transmission duty cycles (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19)).\n\n**Table 2.**Maximum Search Radii of Earth-scale Radio Technosignatures for Current and Future Radio ObservatoriesNarrowband SignalsWideband SignalsTx Antenna (Diameter)DSS-14 (70 m)DSS-14 (70 m)DSS-14 (70 m)DSS-14 (70 m)Operating Mode (Tx Power)DSC (100 kW)RADAR (1 MW)DSC (100 kW)RADAR (1 MW)(lt-yr)(lt-yr)(lt-yr)(lt-yr)ATA414101350ATCA723168550GBT15483631100MeerKAT13463191000ngVLA3911910063200SKA371179503100VLA16553911200\n\nDownload table as:[ASCII](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/0004-637X/957/1/15/suppdata/apjacf56ct2_ascii.txt?doi=10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c)[Typeset image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56ct2_lr.gif)\n\nThe key point illustrated in these figures is given the fixed assumptions for transmit power and antenna size, technosignature footprints of narrowband signals (e.g., interplanetary communications) are much weaker than equivalent wideband signals. Simultaneously, the search radius for narrowband signals is much smaller compared to that of wideband signal observation.\n\nThe data plotted in Figure[5](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf5)translate to the information shown in the second column of Table[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct2). The VLA offers the best search radius, approximately 16 lt-yr, among currently available observatories when searching for a narrowband footprint generated by a DSN-like deep space communications system (70 m dish antenna transmitting using 100 kW of RF power). More sensitive observatories such as the SKA (Braun et al.[2015](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib4)) and ngVLA (Murphy et al.[2018](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib22)) will expand the search radius for observing narrowband transmissions from Earth-scale civilization but still fall short of what is needed for detecting narrowband deep space communications from the closest high-ESI system in our target list, Trappist-1, at 41 lt-yr.\n\nSimilarly, the data from Figure[6](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf6)correspond to the tabular information shown in the rightmost column of Table[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct2). Assuming a technosignature will appear as a wideband signal transmitted using characteristics similar to that operated by the DSN (70 m dish antenna transmitting 1 MW of RF power), the search radii for Earth-scale technosignatures by next-generation observatories will plausibly surpass 3000 lt-yr.\n\nIt should be noted the range limits shown in Table[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct2)are the maximum range values across all frequencies operated by the observatory (i.e., a telescope’s most sensitive channel offers its farthest search radius). For example, in Figures[5](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf5)and[6](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf6), the most sensitive observing frequency of the SKA is just below ∼2 GHz and will be used as the reference point for calculating the SKA’s maximum search radii, shown in Table[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct2). This process is repeated for all variations of the transmission source and all observatories considered, with the maximum search radii for each observatory and transmission type pairing provided in Table[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56ct2).\n\n## 3. Pragmatism of Intercepting Beamed Communications\n\n### 3.1. Planetary Rotation and the Need for Tracked Communications\n\nFor eavesdropping on transmissions not directed at Earth or even intentional communications, how much pointing misalignment can we handle?\n\nWhile other studies have focused on searching for terrestrial-based transmissions from extrasolar planets (Sullivan et al.[1978](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib38); Saide et al.[2023](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib29)), this search framework favors the predictable timing, pointing, and detectability in measuring interplanetary transmissions from Earth-scale civilizations.\n\nThis work justifies why tracked transmissions are essential for generating a detectable technosignature footprint among Earth-scale civilizations.\n\nA pointed, directive, beamed transmission concentrates flux more efficiently as an emitted wave travels through space, but a tracking transmitter allows for detectable technosignature not to be limited in duration by planetary rotation (Cameron[1963](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib5)).\n\nTerrestrial communications from Earth-scale signatures are discounted here as they are not tracked and beamed toward celestial targets. These types of transmission sources are victim to planetary rotation. Detecting directive emissions from a ∼Type 0.7 civilization without the use of a tracking transmit antenna is unlikely. Figure[7](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf7)shows how the Earth illumination from fixed antennas on rotating planets are limited in signal duration, especially for more directive large-aperture transmitters. The case for a fixed transmit frequency of 1 GHz and an exoplanet with a rotation period of 1 day is shown. As larger apertures, and thus tighter beamwidths, are used to transmit a signal from an exoplanet, the detectable signal duration becomes increasingly shortened. This illustration is simply showing the amount of time Earth is within a transmission beam. Accounting for flux and detectability further limits the signal durations illustrated here, but this approach can be used to calculate the upper bounds for the signal duration from untracked transmissions, such as terrestrial signals.\n\n**Figure 7.**Time duration of beamed emission from a fixed transmit antenna on an exoplanet with a 1 day rotation period. Values assume a frequency of 1 GHz. Blue lines represent how long a transmission would illuminate Earth based on the size of the transmit antenna. Orange lines represent how long a transmission would illuminate Earth based on the transmission’s half-power beamwidth. The windowed plot shows the same information on a timescale of 5 minutes, highlighting the shorter observational periods for transmissions from fixed antennas (transmitted without the use of a tracking or active pointing system). For example, a 1000 m2aperture, transmitting from a fixed antenna at 1 GHz on a planet with a rotation period of 1 day, could not illuminate the Earth for greater than 2 minutes unless a tracking mechanism or mount is used. This shortcoming of fixed antennas relative to interplanetary communications and radar justifies why beam-tracking is a crucial component in detecting any directive deep space transmissions. These plots further highlight the temporal benefit of pursuing tracked, interplanetary communications and radar operations with well-defined, hours-long observation windows about secondary eclipses and PPOs.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf7_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf7_hr.jpg)\n\nFortunately, deep space communications and interplanetary radar operations use large, tracked antennas with kilowatts to megawatts of power pointed toward predetermined fixed points in space (Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19); Slobin & Pham[2010](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib34),[2013](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib35); NASA[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib23)).\n\nFor Earth-scale civilizations with RF power capabilities similar to our DSN, higher gain antennas are required to transmit a signal detectable at Earth. In addition to providing a temporal and spatial window for technosignature observation, interplanetary communications and radar require the use of beam-tracking. These tracking antennas allow for more power to be delivered to a fixed point of the sky for hours, hopefully with Earth in the background (illustrated in Figures[2](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf2)and[3](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf3)).\n\n### 3.2. Angular Misalignment\n\nUplink transmissions to satellites in deep space require a high amount of power be delivered to a transmit antenna, which then focuses and emits the energy into free space. Larger-aperture antennas will create more narrow beams and therefore demand better pointing accuracy to deliver adequate power flux density to a satellite. This error, referred to in this work as misalignment, is an aggregate pointing error comprised of the transmitter’s pointing accuracy, the inclination of the exoplanet’s orbit, and the timing error of the orbital model. In other words, the technosignature will be better pointed toward Earth if (1) the transmitter is accurately pointed toward its intended target and (2) the exoplanet the transmission originates from has an orbit with a small grazing angle (i.e., a higher-impact parameter transit). For the case of PPO observation, the timing error inherent in orbital models presents an additional obstacle and constrains such observational geometries. In addition, secondary eclipses are more prevalent than PPOs, and this difficulty in accurately timing PPO observations is one reason secondary eclipse observations are the more pragmatic and achievable approach compared to PPOs.\n\nCombined, these factors create an angular offset between the center of the transmission beam and Earth, hereby referred to as angular misalignment or pointing error. The effect this pointing error has on the detectability of a technosignature is significant. A visualization for the impact of this misalignment between a transmission and Earth is shown in Figure[8](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf8). The figure assumes fixed values for antenna diameter, antenna efficiency, transmission power, and distance. Given these fixed values, a trade study between beamwidth and beam control is illustrated.\n\n**Figure 8.**A visualization for the effect of signal beamwidth and pointing on signal reception. All subfigures assume an antenna diameter of 70 m, 70% antenna efficiency, 100 kW of RF transmit power, and distance of 10 lt-yr. The transmission source is shown as a red circle, the Sun is shown as a yellow circle, and the white outline of the transmitted beam represents the detectable level of flux (the 1*σ*sensitivity limit of the VLA is shown in this example). If the Sun is within the white detection envelope, then enough flux from the technosignature reaches Earth for successful measurement. Top left: visualization of a 1 GHz transmission and the standard DSN pointing error of ∼0004 (NASA[2017](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib23); Slobin & Pham[2013](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib35)); bottom left: a 1 GHz transmission with a pointing error of 0104; top right: a 10 GHz transmission with a pointing error of 0004°; and bottom right: a 10 GHz transmission with a pointing error of 0104. The key takeaway from this figure is that given a fixed value for how large of an antenna is available, higher-frequency operations enable the use of more directive and narrow beamwidths but also demand more precise beam steering as a result. Unless a civilization is intentionally directing transmissions toward Earth, lower-frequency observations are significantly favored. This relationship between the flux at Earth from misaligned Earth-scale technosignatures vs the sensitivities of observatories is presented in Figure[9](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf9).\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf8_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf8_hr.jpg)\n\nFigure[8](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf8)allows us to visualize how our frequency and observational geometry can be limiting factors when eavesdropping the communications and radar of other civilizations. For the fixed antenna size assumed, as frequency increases, the spatial beamwidth of the transmission narrows. However, as the frequency of operation increases, pointing error also becomes less tolerable.\n\nThis effect, visualized for VLA measurements of ∼Type 0.7 (Sagan[2000](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib28)) technosignature footprints, is shown in Figure[8](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf8)at two frequencies and two different pointing scenarios. To see this limitation across a wider range of frequencies and various pointing scenarios across all observatories, please refer to the plot provided in Figure[9](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf9).\n\n**Figure 9.**Effect of angular misalignment on narrowband signal detection. This figure assumes an Earth-scale deep space communications uplink transmission (70 m antenna diameter, 100 kW*RF*transmission power) at a distance of 10 lt-yr from Earth. For these fixed assumptions in antenna size and transmission power, it is clear to see the increasing difficulty for tolerating misalignment when using narrower beamwidths at higher frequencies. Thus, for eavesdropping scenarios where transmissions are not intentionally directed toward Earth, lower-frequency observations should be prioritized before those of higher frequencies.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf9_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/957/1/15/revision1/apjacf56cf9_hr.jpg)\n\nThe example in Figure[9](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cf9)assumes a 70 m antenna diameter, 100 kW*RF*transmission power, and narrowband operation. The flux at Earth is shown for various aggregate angular misalignments (a combination of the standard pointing accuracy of ∼0004 for a 70 m DSN antenna, Mudgway[2001](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib19); Slobin & Pham[2013](https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acf56c#apjacf56cbib35), and the orbital inclinations of the planets observed).\n\nAs the misalignment between the beam center of the technosignature transmission becomes more misaligned with Earth, the case for detection becomes significantly constrained. Only more sensitive observatories will be able to detect more misaligned transmissions. Due to directivity increasing with frequency, more misaligned signals favor observation at lower frequencies.\n\nIt is certainly more pragmatic of a ∼Type 0.7 civilization to prioritize exploring their local star and planetary system before attempting to communicate with Earth. That being the case, the opportunities for eavesdropping on interplanetary communications and radar are bountiful but bounded. Pointing error is a real consideratio", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:23:43.241714+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:faf35c769b003960ae628e46", "attempt_id": "attempt:807d733238d21d47e53fad6c", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5"}, "result": "## COSMIC’s Large-scale Search for Technosignatures during the VLA Sky Survey: Survey Description and First Results - IOPscience\n\n**Source**: https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5\n\n---\n\n[](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5)\n\n[](https://iopscience.iop.org/journal/1538-3881)\n\n[The American Astronomical Society (AAS)](https://aas.org/), established in 1899 and based in Washington, DC, is the major organization of professional astronomers in North America. Its membership of about 7,000 individuals also includes physicists, mathematicians, geologists, engineers, and others whose research and educational interests lie within the broad spectrum of subjects comprising contemporary astronomy. The mission of the AAS is to enhance and share humanity's scientific understanding of the universe.\n\nThe following article isOpen access\n\n# COSMIC’s Large-scale Search for Technosignatures during the VLA Sky Survey: Survey Description and First Results\n\nC. D. Tremblay,J. Sofair,L. Steffes,T. Myburgh,D. Czech,P. B. Demorest,R. A. Donnachie,A. W. Pollak,M. Ruzindana,Siemion A. P. V.\n\nPublished 2025 February 4•© 2025. The Author(s). Published by the American Astronomical Society.\n[The Astronomical Journal](https://iopscience.iop.org/journal/1538-3881),[Volume 169](https://iopscience.iop.org/volume/1538-3881/169),[Number 3](https://iopscience.iop.org/issue/1538-3881/169/3)**Citation**C. D. Tremblay*et al*2025*AJ***169**122**DOI**10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5\n\n[PDFOpens in a new tab.](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5/pdf)\n\n[ePub](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5/epub)\n\nYou need an eReader or compatible software to experience[the benefits of the ePub3 file format](http://iopscience.iop.org/page/ePub3).\n\n## \n\nC. D. Tremblay\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nSETI Institute, 339 Bernardo Avenue, Suite 200, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA\n\nBerkeley SETI Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4409-3515](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4409-3515)J. Sofair\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nLafayette College, Department of Physics, Easton, PA 18045, USA\n\nNational Radio Astronomy Observatory, 1003 Lopezville Road, Socorro, NM 87801, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0009-0002-0688-513X](https://orcid.org/0009-0002-0688-513X)L. Steffes\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nBerkeley SETI Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA\n\nUniversity of Wisconsin–Madison, Department of Astronomy, 475 North Charter Street, Madison, WI 53703, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9512-5492](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9512-5492)T. Myburgh\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nSETI Institute, 339 Bernardo Avenue, Suite 200, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA\n\nMydon Solutions (Pty) Ltd., 102 Silver Oaks, 23 Silverlea Road, Wynberg, Cape Town, 7800, South Africa\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0804-9362](https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0804-9362)D. Czech\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nBerkeley SETI Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8071-6011](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8071-6011)P. B. Demorest\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nNational Radio Astronomy Observatory, 1003 Lopezville Road, Socorro, NM 87801, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6664-965X](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6664-965X)R. A. Donnachie\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nSETI Institute, 339 Bernardo Avenue, Suite 200, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA\n\nMydon Solutions (Pty) Ltd., 102 Silver Oaks, 23 Silverlea Road, Wynberg, Cape Town, 7800, South Africa\n\n[https://orcid.org/0009-0001-8677-372X](https://orcid.org/0009-0001-8677-372X)A. W. Pollak\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nSETI Institute, 339 Bernardo Avenue, Suite 200, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3430-7671](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3430-7671)M. Ruzindana\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nBerkeley SETI Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9473-9652](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9473-9652)Siemion A. P. V.\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nSETI Institute, 339 Bernardo Avenue, Suite 200, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA\n\nBreakthrough Listen, University of Oxford, Department of Physics, Denys Wilkinson Building, Keble Road, Oxford, OX1 3RH, UK\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2828-7720](https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2828-7720)S. S. Varghese\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nSETI Institute, 339 Bernardo Avenue, Suite 200, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2669-0364](https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2669-0364)S. Z. Sheikh\n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nSETI Institute, 339 Bernardo Avenue, Suite 200, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA\n\nBerkeley SETI Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7057-4999](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7057-4999)\n\n## Article metrics\n\n**1756**Total downloads\n**0**Video abstract views\n\n## Share this article\n- [](mailto:?subject=COSMIC’s Large-scale Search for Technosignatures during the VLA Sky Survey: Survey Description and First Results&body=COSMIC’s Large-scale Search for Technosignatures during the VLA Sky Survey: Survey Description and First Results - https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5)\n- [](https://www.linkedin.com/sharing/share-offsite/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiopscience.iop.org%2Farticle%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fad9ea5)\n- [](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fad9ea5)\n- [](https://x.com/share?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fad9ea5&text=COSMIC%E2%80%99s+Large-scale+Search+for+Technosignatures+during+the+VLA+Sky+Survey%3A+Survey+Description+and+First+Results&via=AAS_Publishing)\n- [](https://www.mendeley.com/import/?doi=10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fad9ea5)\n- [](https://bsky.app/intent/compose?text=COSMIC%E2%80%99s+Large-scale+Search+for+Technosignatures+during+the+VLA+Sky+Survey%3A+Survey+Description+and+First+Results%0Ahttps%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fad9ea5%0Avia+%40ioppublishing.bsky.social)\n\n## \n\n## Dates\n- Received2024 September 13\n- Revised2024 December 6\n- Accepted2024 December 11\n- Published2025 February 4\n\n[](https://crossmark.crossref.org/dialog/?doi=10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5&domain=pdf)\n\nUnified Astronomy Thesaurus concepts\n\n[GPU computing](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/1969);[Astrobiology](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/74);[Search for extraterrestrial intelligence](http://astrothesaurus.org/uat/2127)\n\n[Journal RSS](https://iopscience.iop.org/journal/rss/1538-3881)\n\n[Create or edit your corridor alerts](https://myiopscience.iop.org/signin?origin=a0&idhub=true&return=https%3A%2F%2Fiopscience.iop.org%2Farticle%2F10.3847%2F1538-3881%2Fad9ea5)\n\n[What are corridors?](https://journals.aas.org/topical-corridors/)\n\n1538-3881/169/3/122\n\n## Abstract\n\nDeveloping algorithms to search through data efficiently is a challenging part of searching for signs of technology beyond our solar system. We have built a digital signal processing system and computer cluster on the backend of the Karl G. Jansky Very Large Array (VLA) in New Mexico in order to search for signals throughout the Galaxy consistent with our understanding of artificial radio emissions. In our first paper, we described the system design and software pipelines. In this paper, we describe a postprocessing pipeline to identify persistent sources of interference, filter out false positives, and search for signals not immediately identifiable as anthropogenic radio frequency interference during the VLA Sky Survey. As of 2024 September 1, the Commensal Open-source Multi-mode Interferometric Cluster had observed more than 950,000 unique pointings. This paper presents the strategy we employ when commensally observing during the VLA Sky Survey and a postprocessing strategy for the data collected during the survey. To test this postprocessing pipeline, we searched toward 511 stars from the Gaia catalog with coherent beams. This represents about 30 minutes of observation during the VLA Sky Survey, where we typically observe about 2000 sources hr–1in the coherent beamforming mode. We did not detect any unidentifiable signals, setting isotropic power limits ranging from 1011to 1016W.\n\nExport citation and abstract[BibTeX](https://iopscience.iop.org/export?type=article&doi=10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5&exportFormat=iopexport_bib&exportType=abs&navsubmit=Export+abstract)[RIS](https://iopscience.iop.org/export?type=article&doi=10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5&exportFormat=iopexport_ris&exportType=abs&navsubmit=Export+abstract)\n\n[**Previous**article in issue](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ada14c)\n\n[**Next**article in issue](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ada4b4)\n\n## \n\n- [NASA ADS Record](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025AJ....169..122T)\n- [Simbad Objects](https://simbad.u-strasbg.fr/simbad/sim-ref?querymethod=bib&simbo=on&bibcode=2025AJ....169..122T)\n- [About Related Links](https://iopscience.iop.org/journal/1538-3881/page/About%20Related%20Links)\n\nOriginal content from this work may be used under the terms of the[Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.\n\nThis article was updated on April 7, 2025 to correct a production error which led to incorrect text citations for some references.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe place of humanity in the Universe and the existence of life is one of the most profound and widespread questions in astronomy and society in general. Throughout history, humans have marveled at the starry night sky. It is intriguing to consider the distances to the nearest stars, the origins of our Universe, and how life on Earth came into existence. During the past 60 yr, the active search for life on other planets has not resulted in any evidence that it has occurred (e.g., S. Z. Sheikh et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib32); S. Smith et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib36); P. X. Ma et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib23)). Even the long-debated “Wow” signal (J. Ehman[2008](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib8)) is no longer thought to be related to extraterrestrial life (A. Méndez et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib26)and references therein). Therefore, we continue to wonder whether life is truly confined to this small planet orbiting an ordinary main-sequence star.\n\nStarting in 2022, we built the Commensal Open-source Multi-mode Interferometric Cluster (COSMIC; J. Hickish et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib14); C. D. Tremblay et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)), offering the first Ethernet-based digital framework on the world-leading Karl G. Jansky Very Large Array (VLA) in New Mexico for rapid, real-time analysis of astronomy data. COSMIC was designed to provide an autonomous real-time pipeline for observing and processing data for one of the largest experiments in the search for extraterrestrial intelligence to date. To meet this design goal, we have constructed a digital signal processing system and computer cluster that takes a copy of each of the digitized antenna voltages, corrects the signals, calibrates the gains, coherently beamforms on targets of interest, forms an incoherent sum, and then searches for signals of a spectral and temporal nature consistent with our understanding of artificial technological signals (~Hz wide emission signals; technosignatures). The output of this search is a database of “hits” and small cutouts of the phase-corrected voltage data for each antenna around the hits called “postage stamps.”\n\nThis is an opportune time for COSMIC to become operational, as it is possible to observe concurrently with the VLA Sky Survey (VLASS; M. Lacy et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib19)). VLASS observes the entire northern hemisphere over two semesters in the VLA B-configuration, where Epoch 3 of the survey started in 2023 January. Since 2023 March, COSMIC has been operational and recording data from all 27 antennas with up to 1.2 GHz of bandwidth. The survey continued recording data from the second half of the sky starting in 2024 May to complete a full census at decl. above –40°. Observing along with this survey allows us to conduct one of the largest technosignature searches ever attempted within the frequency range of 2–4 GHz, covering almost 1 million stars within 2 yr.\n\nThe vast amount of data generated by the software pipelines running on COSMIC and other similar backends has led to the development of fast and efficient methods for locating narrowband and Doppler drifting signals. In S. Z. Sheikh et al. ([2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib33)), the authors discuss this problem in detail and conclude that the tree summation algorithm (also known as the Taylor tree method) is the most commonly implemented in modern technosignature searches due to its efficiency. However, the FastDD method (K. M. Houston[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib15)) offers some promising improvements. The main software program used by the community employing the tree summation method to date is TURBOSETI (E. Enriquez & D. Price[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib9)),[8](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn1)which is used on observations from the Robert C. Byrd Green Bank Telescope (e.g., V. Gajjar et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib11); C. Choza et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib3)), the Parkes 64 m Telescope (e.g., D. C. Price et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib31); S. Z. Sheikh et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib32)), the Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical radio Telescope (e.g., Z.-Z. Tao et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib37); Y.-C. Wang et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib40)), and the Low-Frequency Array (O. A. Johnson et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib18)).\n\nWithin the pipelines running on COSMIC, we use SETICORE,[9](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn2)which is a GPU-accelerated implementation of TURBOSETI written in C++ with additional features: it includes postage-stamp extraction from the phase-corrected voltages of each antenna and visualization tools. This software is also implemented in the commensal technosignature search experiment on the MeerKAT telescope (D. Czech et al. 2024, in preparation). As with TURBOSETI, the user input parameters include the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) threshold and the Doppler acceleration drift rate.[10](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn3)It is possible to determine the precise expected drift rate based on the planet's orbital motion (J.-K. Li et al.[2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib20); M. G. Li et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib22)), but this is only applicable to targeted surveys of known planetary systems. When detecting signals from nearby stars where planets are likely to exist but have not yet been discovered, as with COSMIC, broad search criteria are used. To search the data, we use an SNR threshold of 8 and a Doppler acceleration range of ±50 Hz s−1. This limit is much broader than necessary for known exoplanets (S. Z. Sheikh et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib33); M. G. Li et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib22)) but accounts for a broader range of possible scenarios where technology is orbiting off the planet but within the solar system (like Earth's satellites). Coherently and incoherently beamformed data are searched using these parameters, with a frequency resolution of 7.63 Hz channel–1for the total 8 s recording segment, resulting in a drift rate bin size of 0.95 Hz s−1. We also collect and save data with a drift rate of exactly 0 Hz s−1. Such data are often discarded in many experiments as a way to limit the intrusion of terrestrial signals into the data. However, the 0.95 Hz s−1may not provide the necessary resolution to detect signals arriving from systems similar to our nearest neighbors, such as TRAPPIST-1 g (1.33 Hz s−1) or Kepler-438 b (0.7090 Hz s−1), as calculated by J.-K. Li et al. ([2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib20)).\n\nOnce we have these detected signals and their associated metadata collected into a database, we need to devise plans to sort through these data to eliminate false positives likely associated with terrestrial radio emitters or instrumental artifacts and find new signals with astronomical origins. Although many of the experiments published so far involved manually investigating the dynamic spectra of the most promising events, this is no longer practical where the COSMIC pipeline identifies millions of signals per hour over thousands of sources. Although observational changes such as looking at signals that traverse across a multibeam receiver (i.e., B.-L. Huang et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib16)) or flagging known terrestrial signals within the real-time pipeline could be considered in the future, other ways of evaluating the data are required to investigate the data collected in the first 6 months of operation with COSMIC on the VLA.\n\nIn this paper, we describe a postprocessing strategy for the data collected and processed by COSMIC during VLASS observations. In Section[2](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5s2), COSMIC's observations during VLASS are summarized, and the observations that have been evaluated are described. In Section[3](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5s3), we discuss the effects of a radio frequency interference (RFI) excision technique used after the data have been collected on COSMIC. In Sections[4](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5s4)and[5](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5s5), we discuss a postprocess for the evaluation of COSMIC-generated data for SETI and the first results from one of the VLASS observation nights. Finally, we discuss the impact of the results and conclusions of this work.\n\n## 2. Observations\n\nThe observations described in this paper are based on data taken simultaneously with other VLA observing projects but analyzed separately, following the relevant National Radio Astronomy Observatory (NRAO) policies[11](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn4)for commensal observation. Therefore, the COSMIC team does not control the observational strategy or calibration cadence. Our processing and analysis do not impact the standard NRAO operation and analysis used by the primary observer or the NRAO staff.\n\n### 2.1. VLASS Observing Strategy\n\nDuring VLASS, we observed and recorded data while the VLA was in the B-configuration and the hybrid configuration called B-North-A. Two observation campaigns, one in 2023 and one in 2024, represent the third epoch of this survey, which covers 80% of the sky. As part of this survey, large sections of the sky are rapidly covered by the telescope using the on-the-fly (OTF) mosaic method.[12](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn5)During this time, observations are broken into segments called “tiles.” Each tile covers between 25 and 40 deg2by moving in R.A. at a rate of 33 s–1, meaning that there are approximately 5–10 s on each source before the source is outside the primary beam (the slew rate is slower at decl. below ~–20°). After scanning in R.A. for approximately 10 minutes, the telescope moves in decl. by 72 and then scans again in R.A., covering the same distance as the previous track. Overall, it takes about 2 hr to observe each tile. For further details on the observation, see M. Lacy et al. ([2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib19)).\n\nFor COSMIC, we adopted an observing strategy where voltages are recorded in 8 s increments and processed separately. As described in C. D. Tremblay et al. ([2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)), the targets on which to form coherent beams, phase-centered pointing toward particular sources with a field diameter equal to the point-spread function, were selected based on the sources likely to spend the most time within the half-power point of the telescope's primary beam when a fully recorded field of view (FOV) is considered. The list of targets is from the technosignature target catalog, which itself is a selection of sources based on the Gaia Space Telescope DR2 catalog (D. Czech et al.[2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib6)). For each 2 hr observation of a single tile, the COSMIC software pipelines forms approximately 4000 coherent beams on sources in the target catalog.\n\nDuring VLASS Epoch 3.1 in 2023, COSMIC observed and recorded science-ready data from 2023 March 25 to 2023 June 15, as shown in Figure[1](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5f1). We accumulated 326,504 unique target fields (unique R.A. and decl. coordinates), representing 267,510 stars toward which we formed coherent beams, and 359,353,639 “hits” found by SETICORE. To assess the strategy and data quality, we used 30 minutes of data observed on 2023 April 15 during the recording of a partial VLASS tile. The VLA was in B-configuration and recording data at 2–4 GHz in the 8 bit recording mode, and 24 of the 27 antennas were online and recording for the duration of the observation. Every 15 minutes, the VLA conducted phase calibration scans, with at least one scan of 3C 286 per tile used for flux calibration.\n\n**Figure 1.**A plot of all the coordinates of targeted stars during VLASS recordings from 2023 March 25 to 2023 June 15. All recorded data of scientific quality that are contained with the COSMIC database for Epoch 3.1 are represented here.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f1_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f1_hr.jpg)\n\n### 2.2. COSMIC Recording and Processing Setup\n\nCOSMIC is divided into four distinct segments (digital signal processing, calibration, beamforming, and search), the last three of which take place in real time and are outlined in Figure[2](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5f2)and described in detail in C. D. Tremblay et al. ([2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)). Digital signals are received and processed by the first rack of COSMIC. Each field programmable gate array (FPGA) board receives the digital signals from two of the antenna's digital transmission streams using existing VLA firmware provided by NRAO (but processes each independently). They also\n- \n\n1.\n\ncompensate for delays due to both signal propagation from the astronomical source of interest to the VLA antennas and fiber optic signal propagation from the antennas to the COSMIC system,\n- \n\n2.\n\nremove local oscillator offsets and create coarsely channelized 1 MHz channels from the broadband input, and\n- \n\n3.\n\nform packets of digitized coarse channels to transmit via a 100 Gb Ethernet switch to a runtime-determined downstream processing node (the GPU node).\n\nIncoming data from the VLA consist of two intermediate frequencies (IFs) of 1024 MHz each, for a total of 2048 MHz, when in the 8 bit recording mode. During the 2023 VLASS observations, we had up to 15 operational GPU servers (some shut down as we worked on system stability), each containing two processing nodes. In order to search for signals within the 960 MHz of bandwidth, we used the 100 Gb switch to transfer 32 × 1 MHz coarse channels to each node, which were then split between the two IFs (480 MHz each) between the 2 and 4 GHz that the COSMIC system ingested. As shown in Figure[3](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5f3), there was significant RFI on the edges of the band, so we focused the processing and search on 2.5–3.5 GHz, as will be further discussed in Section[3](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5s3).\n\n**Figure 2.**Summary of the pipeline implemented in COSMIC. Based on the telescope's observational intent, the data will either flow from the hardware to the calibration pipeline or from the hardware to the search pipeline on the GPUs. The postprocessing step, which will be described later in this work, is a separate data flow and does not happen in real time. A more detailed depiction of the process can be found in Figures 3 and 8 of C. D. Tremblay et al. ([2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)).\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f2_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f2_hr.jpg)\n\n**Figure 3.**Histogram showing the number of frequencies per frequency window that have high kurtosis, implying high levels of RFI. The edges at 2–2.5 GHz and 3–3.5 GHz show significant RFI. Therefore, for VLASS, the central frequency range of 2.5–3.5 GHz was processed and searched.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f3_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f3_hr.jpg)\n\nEach 32 MHz of bandwidth is processed through either the calibration pipeline or the technosignature search pipeline. The VLA observations are set with a series of “intents,”[13](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn6)which are assigned by the person creating the scheduling block. When the intent contains the word “calibration,” the data are processed through the calibration pipeline, as defined in the second line of Figure[2](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5f2). When the intent is “OBSERVE TARGET,” the data are run through the technosignature search pipeline. The following sections describe these processes in detail. The information about what the pipeline detected is recorded to an SQL database, and “postage stamps” of the calibrated voltages are saved on disk.\n\nThe data from the 2023 April 15 observations include coherent beamformed results on 511 unique Gaia DR2 sources from the catalog created by D. Czech et al. ([2021](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib6)), some of which were searched for drifting signals multiple times when the decl. strips overlapped with a previous slew of the telescope. We also searched within the incoherent sum in each 8 s of data.\n\n### 2.3. Calibration\n\nThe COSMIC processing pipeline follows standard calibration procedures for radio astronomy data with a strong focus on delay and phase calibration. Currently, the automated pipeline does not conduct absolute flux density calibration. However, the data are saved on disk, and this can be applied to the data prior to reporting any values. The pipeline uses the XGPU[14](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn7)(M. A. Clark et al.[2013](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib4)) software correlator to cross-correlate the raw voltages and create the visibility data products in four polarizations (RR, LL, RL, and LR, where R and L refer to right and left circular polarization, respectively), which are saved in*uvh*5 files[15](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn8)on the storage nodes. Once each of the 32 MHz of data is correlated and the delays and gain solutions are computed, the data are collated back together across all frequencies on the COSMIC head (computer) node to be reorganized and reported. The new residual delay and phase solutions are uploaded to the FPGAs, and the resultant solutions are saved in a*json*file format in a folder with the*uvh*5 file for posterity.\n\nAs part of the calibration process, after the solutions are brought together and sorted by frequency, the solution quality is graded based on the stability of the phases. With each new calibration processing pipeline application, the FPGA signal corrections get updated. Therefore, each new calibration has had some calibration applied, and the results of the phase are really a difference from the previous calibration. The main grading criterion we use to define a good calibration compared to a bad calibration is phase stability. When the phases are flat across frequencies (per each polarization and antenna), the resultant grade is given the value of 1. When the phases are chaotic, uncorrelated, or contain significant wraps, the grade value decreases toward 0. See Equation (9) in C. D. Tremblay et al. ([2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)) for details on how the overall grade is computed in detail.\n\nUncorrelated individual frequency channels or antennas, often caused by an incorrect delay model or the antenna still slewing during recording, can corrupt the overall grade value. That is why, in addition to the overall grade, we compute a grade per frequency channel across antennas (Equation (8) in C. D. Tremblay et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)) and a grade per antenna across all frequency channels (Equation (7) in C. D. Tremblay et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)) to better isolate these instances. However, these grades are determined before the calibration is applied and representative of a differentiation between calibration runs more than an accurate measure of the determined calibration values for the current observation.[16](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn9)\n\nExamples of the calibration phases plotted as phase degrees versus frequency are shown in Figure[4](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5f4). The overall calibration grade and the plots, along with other diagnostic plots, are sent to a Slack[17](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn10)channel for posterity and to allow those reviewing the data for scientific use a rapid review and evaluation of the calibration quality during the observations of interest. When a calibration grade is above a value of 0.6, representing that there is no more than a 40% drop in sensitivity, and chosen as a value suggestive that all but one or two antennas have phases that are easy to calibrate, no further investigation of the solutions is completed. The resultant science data proceeding the calibration are taken as worthy of investigation. When the score is less than 0.6, the plots and other diagnostics are reviewed to see if the data are scientifically valid. These phase solutions are critical; in coherent beamforming, improper phase solutions will cause the coherent beam to be offset from the position of the target source, creating either a significant reduction in sensitivity or a smeared point-spread function (depending on the severity of the problem).\n\n**Figure 4.**Example calibration plots from the AC tuning generated by plotting the phases prior to the calibration being applied. In (a), the overall grade is <0.2, (b) has a score of 0.639, and (c) has a score of 0.991. A score greater than 0.6 is considered a “good” calibration, as this represents a score prior to the correction to the FPGA. Therefore, it shows the ease with which a solution can be found more than the actual solution. The red vertical strips indicate 1 MHz channels that are flagged as containing RFI signals during the calibration run.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f4_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f4_hr.jpg)\n\nIn the last year, we improved the phase solution calculation by flagging for 1 MHz channels subject to narrowband RFI. For the spectrum of a given tuning, baseline, and polarization averaged over time, a channel that satisfies\n\nis considered to contain RFI, where*f*is the frequency,*i*is the channel index, and MAD is the median absolute deviation. A flagged spectral value*f**i*is replaced with the median (*f*) so as not corrupt the calculated gains and, in turn, the applied phase solution. We note that while RFI emission is largely coherent, it is not usually continuous, and so deriving gains from RFI-contaminated signals will likely lead to gain solutions for that channel that do not accurately represent the scientific data in the target observation.\n\nFor longevity and retrospective analysis, two calibration tables in the COSMIC database are created. They are “AntennaCalibration” and “ObservationCalibration.” In the “ObservationCalibration” table, we store the overall calibration details linked to the calibration observation. This is mostly used for grade performance evaluation in time studies. “ObservationCalibration” stores the calibration observation ID, the reference antenna, the overall grade, and the output directory for the calibration observation and its products. “AntennaCalibration” stores the antenna and tuning-specific calibration details, which are used to further inspect irregularities noted in grade evaluation studies. For each antenna entry, while linked to the calibration observation ID, we store the antenna name, tuning, number of coarse channels processed in the calibration run, and number of RFI-flagged channels during the calibration run.\n\nTo evaluate the stability of the calibration score and COSMIC's pipeline operation, we plotted the data from 2023 May and June. The most common calibrators used by observers and recorded by COSMIC were J2355+4950, J1024–0052, J1033+6051, and 3C 286. As demonstrated in the plot in Figure[5](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5f5), more than 80% of the calibrations across a range of frequencies have a score of greater than 0.6. In particular, the best scores are often from strong phase and flux calibrators used frequently in the calibration of VLA data. Many of the poor-quality data plots (in blue) are often from weak phase calibrators chosen by the observer or due to a frequency change with respect to previous observations.\n\n**Figure 5.**Calibration results for 2 months of operation. The colors of the bubbles represent the grade of the calibration, where the higher value is a lower SNR across the phase and the size of the bubble represents the number of times the source was observed. As can be seen, the calibration is good most of the time.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f5_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f5_hr.jpg)\n\nFor the observations reviewed here from 2023 April 15, the grade of the observations varied significantly, but most were in the range of 0.4–0.9. All corrected gain plots show good phase stability for at least 24 antennas.\n\n### 2.4. Beamforming during OTF Scans\n\nAlthough the beamforming code and science verification are discussed in depth in C. D. Tremblay et al. ([2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)), here we discuss their application when the telescope is using the OTF scanning mode as used during VLASS. The COSMIC pipeline uses the beamforming code “Breakthrough Listen Accelerated DSP Engine” (BLADE),[18](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn11)which was developed as part of the refurbishment of the Allen Telescope Array in California, USA.\n\nThe data recorded during an OTF scan are partitioned into files spanning 8.338608 s each. The OTF observation system ensures a constant phase across each file by instructing the antenna to only update phase centers on file boundaries. This limit is also determined as the upper limit after which beamforming and dedoppler search cannot be achieved within “real time.” The coordinates of the beams formed are sourced from candidates within the half-power point of the FOV around the phase center as received from the target selector system (see Figure 16 in C. D. Tremblay et al.[2024](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib39)). Consequently, the beam coordinates are independently derived from each 8 s partition of the data. Before the actual beamforming computation, each coarse channel is upchannelized to the requested resolution without reducing the number of fine spectra below 50 (typically 64 time steps for VLASS observations with an FFT size of 131,072), to the benefit of the dedoppler search, and while keeping the overall processing time within “real-time” bounds. The upchannelization exhausts all time samples for the coarse channel before iterating to the subsequent coarse channel. The following beamforming computation processes each fine spectrum. We collect the whole time span before we do a dedoppler search. Therefore, the dedoppler search is computed iteratively across each upchannelized, beamformed coarse channel, repeating for each time partition of the OTF scan.\n\nTo ensure that the beamformer coefficients are updated appropriately, an observation of the methanol maser emission at the star-forming region W51 (e.g., S. Etoka et al.[2012](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib10)) was taken on 2023 May 31 using the same OTF parameters as VLASS, and 3C 286 was used to calibrate the data through the standard pipeline. By plotting the time samples as a function of normalized beam power for the frequency channel that contains the peak emission of the maser, we see a pattern that mimics the primary beam response of the VLA dishes as expected, shown in Figure[6](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5f6)[19](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn12)(R. Perley[2016](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib30)).\n\n**Figure 6.**The plot above shows the power over time for the frequency channel containing the peak intensity of the W51 6.7 GHz methanol maser emission. The shape of the line is expected to correspond to the antenna's primary beam response. As a comparison, the power response of coherent and incoherent beams (multiplied by a factor of 10 to improve visibility) is plotted, along with their ratio. The ratio is ideally expected to equal the number of antennas used in the summation, which was 21 in this test. Imperfect calibration reduces the ratio away from the ideal value. The high scatter of the ratios away from the peak is due to large errors from small, noisy values.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f6_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f6_hr.jpg)\n\n### 2.5. Real-time Search Pipeline\n\nEach 8 s segment of recorded data is searched for narrowband signals following beamforming. For this, we use the software package SETICORE, which uses the same Taylor tree method as TURBOSETI but is rewritten for GPU acceleration. In the pipeline running COSMIC during VLASS, SETICORE searches for narrowband signals that have an SNR above 10 and across a drift rate of ±50 Hz s−1. For each signal it detects, the metadata about the signal characteristics are logged into an SQL database, and a segment of the calibrated and channelized voltages is saved as “postage stamps.”\n\nTo test the efficiency of the real-time pipeline at detecting technosignatures, we pointed the telescope toward the Voyager I space satellite using VLASS-style observations. We then let the real-time pipeline run with no input except to prioritize a coherent beam toward Voyager's location. As shown in Figure[7](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5f7), SETICORE recorded information on 4121 signals. Voyager's signal around 8420 MHz was one of these signals detected. We note that additional testing will be discussed in a future publication with the full VLASS data release.\n\n**Figure 7.**A plot of the SNR vs. observing frequency showing all of the hits found in the observations toward Voyager detected by SETICORE.\n\nDownload figure:[Standard image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f7_lr.jpg)[High-resolution image](https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/1538-3881/169/3/122/revision2/ajad9ea5f7_hr.jpg)\n\n## 3. RFI\n\nAs mentioned in Section[1](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5s1), the COSMIC postprocessing pipeline searches for signals that are similar to anthropogenic sources of radio emissions in their spectral and temporal signatures. Despite providing a valuable reference for the characteristics of technosignatures, human-made radio emissions are also one of radio astronomy's greatest hindrances. Given the ubiquity of anthropogenic radio emission technology (e.g., cellular networks, satellite radio, etc.), any radio telescope located near enough to humans will detect these unwanted radio signals. For COSMIC, this means signals that do not originate from the target source will be picked up and flagged as potential technosignatures. The signals that cause these false positives are known as RFI. We therefore have created a software package described below to identify prominent and persistent sources of RFI during calibrator observations. The assumption here is that the signals detected in a short observation toward the calibrator are all from sources of terrestrial radio emission and not signals of interest.\n\n### 3.1. Classification and Identification\n\nTwo characteristics of the technosignatures we are seeking are that they could be narrowband (signal contained in ~Hz wide channels) and that their frequency would change over time as a result of the Doppler effect (S. Z. Sheikh et al.[2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib33); J.-K. Li et al.[2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib20)). Technosignatures can be distinguished from typical astronomical sources by their widths in frequency space. The finest-frequency astronomical emitters discovered thus far are found to produce signal widths on the order of 102Hz (M. Gray[1999](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib12); Z.-S. Zhang et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib44)).\n\nRFI permeates astronomical data, causing narrowband spikes in detected power with time-varying intensities and frequency instabilities.[20](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn13),[21](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn14)The similarities between terrestrial radio signals and technosignatures make it challenging to determine if the source of a signal is of extraterrestrial origin or not. Contemporary technosignature search algorithms therefore output a large number of false positives. Thus, the search for an extraterrestrial needle in a “cosmic haystack” (J. H. Wolfe et al.[1981](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib42); J. T. Wright et al.[2018](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib43)) becomes exponentially more challenging in the presence of RFI. Even if a signal can be confirmed to be anthropogenic, its highly variable properties make it troublesome to remove.\n\nCurrently, technosignature searches employ a variety of RFI removal techniques, including observational strategies and postobservation data analysis. Observational techniques include the use of cadence-based observation (some time pointing away from the primary source) for single-dish telescopes (e.g., D. C. Price et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib31)) and the analysis of for which beams—coherent and/or incoherent—a signal is found for interferometers (demonstrated in this work). However, multitelescope follow-up is also a viable and suggested method for signal confirmation (i.e., O. A. Johnson et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib18); P. X. Ma et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib23)). The identification of terrestrial radio signals includes the analysis of*χ*2statistics (R. Weber et al.[1997](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib41)), the use of machine learning (Z.-S. Zhang et al.[2020](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib44); P. X. Ma et al.[2023](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib23)), RFI subtraction using a reference signal (F. H. Briggs et al.[2000](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib2)), and much more. Many of the techniques that fit into the latter category are mathematically intense and/or computationally expensive. Here we seek to develop a new method to swiftly flag frequency ranges that are heavily tainted by RFI to allow for more efficient data analysis.\n\n### 3.2. RFI Identification with Excess Kurtosis\n\nOur first line of defense against RFI uses the statistical measure of excess kurtosis,*exkurt*, which gives the measure of the infrequent outliers in a distribution. We use the Fisher[22](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn15)definition of kurtosis,*kurt*, in this work, given by\n\nwhere*μ*4is the fourth central moment of the distribution and*μ*2is the second central moment (variance). Under this definition, a standard normal distribution has an excess kurtosis of 0.\n\nThe astronomical noise in our data is expected to have power normally distributed in any given frequency bin due to the incoherence of the electromagnetic waves collected by the VLA (E. Smith et al.[2022](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib35)). Therefore, large enough deviations from Gaussianity imply the presence of a signal. We can use this to our advantage to rapidly flag frequency bins that are dominated by RFI, or “dirty.” To filter out the false positives in the database during data postprocessing, we developed a new PYTHON package called the Categorization of RFI in COSMIC with Kurtosis for Extraterrestrial Searches (CRICKETS).[23](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn16)\n\nThe use of excess kurtosis to flag RFI-dominated frequencies is not a novel technique. G. M. Nita & D. E. Gary ([2010](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib27)) proposed the spectral kurtosis estimator,, a mathematical object used to flag RFI within the context of radio astronomy. This estimator was generalized by G. M. Nita et al. ([2019](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5bib28)) and implemented in HYPERSETI,[24](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn17)a PYTHON package designed to search spectral data for technosignatures in both single-dish and interferometric data. COSMIC currently runs a different technosignature search package (SETICORE) that does not have a robust RFI flagging method. The lack of this key function in the COSMIC beamforming and search real-time pipeline is the primary motivation behind the use of CRICKETS within the pipelines running on COSMIC.\n\n### 3.3. CRICKETS Application\n\nFrequency, power, and time data are fed to CRICKETS in the form of a*filterbank*file generated from forming an incoherent beam on a VLA observation toward a calibrator source. This was done with the assumption that no technosignatures would be in the field surrounding the calibrator and that the emission detected in the data would be dominated by broadband emission from the bright calibrator source and RFI. CRICKETS reads these filterbank files in using the BLIMPY[25](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn18)PYTHON package. Once the file is loaded in, the power is averaged over the time axis and tabulated with their respective frequencies.\n\nThe next operations that CRICKETS performs are to organize the data to a user-specified number of frequency bins, rescale the data[26](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5fn19)by a factor of 109, and calculate the excess kurtosis (Equation ([1](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ad9ea5#ajad9ea5eqn1))) of every frequency bin using SCIPY. The excess kurtosis calculation is computed using all of the fine-frequency channels within a given bin. Bins that are dominated by RFI are flagged based on their", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:23:50.418357+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:e992c9ed648746266ea474be", "attempt_id": "attempt:807d733238d21d47e53fad6c", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://www.seti.org/projects/technosignatures-sag"}, "result": "## Technosignatures SAG\n\n**Source**: https://www.seti.org/projects/technosignatures-sag\n\n---\n\n[](https://www.seti.org/projects/technosignatures-sag)[Cancel](https://www.seti.org/projects/technosignatures-sag)\n\n##### Projects\n\n# Technosignatures SAG\n\nTechnosignatures SAG is a group of volunteer experts who have been chartered by NASA to produce a report to NASA’s Exoplanet Exploration Program, containing the results of its analysis on how NASA can better integrate technosignature search into its portfolio.[Technosignatures SAG](https://www.seti.org/search-by-tags/?tag=Technosignatures SAG)[Technosignatures](https://www.seti.org/search-by-tags/?tag=Technosignatures)[Sofia Sheikh](https://www.seti.org/search-by-tags/?tag=Sofia Sheikh)[Steve Croft](https://www.seti.org/search-by-tags/?tag=Steve Croft)\n\n## What is a SAG?\n\n“The ExoPAG is an open, interdisciplinary forum that provides a conduit for community input into NASA’s Exoplanet Exploration Program (ExEP) and NASA’s Astrophysics Division (APD). It also provides an environment for soliciting and coordinating community analysis and input in support of ExEP objectives and their implications for architecture planning and activity prioritization and for future exploration.” [ExoPAG Operating Procedures document]\n\n“SAG” stands for “Study Analysis Group,” which “may be formed adjacent to the ExoPAG to address specific scientific topics of interest or goals [...] SAGs contribute to the ExoPAG’s overall mission and objectives through their specialized focus and expertise.” [ExoPAG Operating Procedures document]\n\n## What is the Technosignature SAG?\n\nIn short, the Technosignatures SAG is a group of volunteer experts who have been chartered by NASA to produce a report to NASA’s Exoplanet Exploration Program, containing the results of its analysis on how NASA can better integrate technosignature search into its portfolio.\n\nThe Technosignature SAG is led by[Sofia Sheikh](https://www.seti.org/people/sofia-sheikh/), and is composed of members, who have volunteered to participate, and a Steering Committee, who have volunteered to provide substantial effort and/or guidance and oversight. The Steering Committee currently consists of Daniel Angerhausen,[Steve Croft](https://www.seti.org/people/steve-croft/), Mark Elowitz, Benjamin Fields, Adam Frank, Megan Li, Eddie Schwieterman, and Jason Wright.\n\n## Charter for SAG-25\n\nThe search for signs of technological life in the universe (\"technosignatures”), also called SETI, has seen rapid growth in its sophistication in recent years, while expanding beyond radio SETI into searches for other forms of technology, such as atmospheric technosignatures in exoplanetary spectra. Importantly, many of the same techniques currently used for biosignature\n\nstudies, including atmospheric modeling and retrieval and standards for life detection, can also be applied to technosignature searches. As such, there are strong and growing connections between technosignature studies and the fields of exoplanetary research and astrobiology.\n\nThe field of astrobiology has been nurtured and matured by NASA for decades, so that it now has a robust portfolio of the study of life in the universe across the Earth Science, Planetary Science, and Astronomy communities. This support has been catalyzed by the discovery of ubiquitous terrestrial planets and the near-term possibility of measuring the surface and atmospheric properties of potentially habitable worlds. NASA has an opportunity to similarly support the small but vibrant and growing community of technosignature researchers who seek to make NASA’s astrobiology portfolio more comprehensive and inclusive of the search for technological life. Indeed, as has been demonstrated, there is no a priori reason to exclude technosignatures as they may be more abundant, long lived, and less ambiguous than biosignatures.\n\nThe scope of the SAG will be searches for technosignatures beyond Earth and its atmosphere, including the solar system and beyond, especially via means familiar to astronomers and planetary scientists. This SAG will produce analysis and findings on how NASA's efforts in astrobiology, exoplanetary astrophysics, and other areas can better serve the search for technosignatures, consistent with NASA's goals to understand the prevalence of life in the Universe.\n\nSpecifically, over its two year anticipated length (~2024–2026) the SAG will:\n- Investigate which technosignature investigations should play a role in NASA's exoplanet and planetary science portfolios.\n- Identify the most promising technosignature investigations to prioritize for funding and mission development.\n- Identify science and technology gaps relevant to technosignature searches.\n- Identify existing NASA programs most relevant to technosignature searches, especially those in exoplanets and planetary science, and determine how they can be more responsive to and supportive of technosignature searches.\n- Identify best practices for NASA program officers to select and provide guidance for review panelists to grade and discuss technosignature proposals in XRP and other programs.\n- Identify potential new initiatives to build community and intercommunications with other astrobiology efforts.\n\n## Other resources\n\nHere is our[Code of Conduct](https://www.seti.org/media/p1mkov1u/technosignatures-study-analysis-group-code-of-conduct.pdf). The indication of interest deadline for SAG-25 has already passed. Other questions and comments can be directed to[[email protected]](https://www.seti.org/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#ea9e8f8982848599838d848b9e9f988f99b5998b8daa998f9e83c485988d).\n\n## Join our newsletter", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:23:56.532789+00:00"}]}}
